/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/google-cdl.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("google-cdl", [["cdl1",0,1,"An online ticket seller gets ten times its normal traffic for a few hours when a big concert goes on sale, then traffic drops back. Which cloud characteristic helps most?",["Fixed capacity sized for the average day, which keeps monthly spending predictable","Elasticity: capacity is added for the spike and released afterward","Buying enough servers for the peak and leaving them idle the rest of the time","A longer contract with a colocation provider for extra rack space"],1,"Elasticity lets capacity grow and shrink automatically with demand, so the seller pays for the spike only while it lasts. Sizing for the average would fail at peak, and buying for the peak leaves expensive hardware idle.","Section 1.1",["Capacity sized for the average day would be overwhelmed by a tenfold spike, so customers would see errors during the sale.",null,"Buying for the peak works technically, but the hardware sits idle most of the year, which is the waste elasticity avoids.","More colocation rack space is still hardware you buy and manage; it cannot appear for a few hours and then disappear."],1],["cdl2",0,1,"A finance director wants to stop making large up-front hardware purchases every three years and pay each month for the IT resources actually used. What change is this?",["Shifting spending from operating expenditure to capital expenditure","Turning variable IT costs into fixed long-term costs","Increasing the depreciation schedule on owned servers","Shifting spending from capital expenditure to operating expenditure"],3,"Up-front purchases of depreciating assets are CapEx; paying for use as you go is OpEx. The cloud moves most IT spending from CapEx to OpEx and turns fixed costs into variable ones, not the other way round.","Section 1.2",["This is the reverse: buying hardware up front is capital expenditure, and paying monthly for use is operating expenditure.","Cloud pay-as-you-go does the opposite, turning fixed hardware costs into variable costs that follow usage.","Depreciation applies to owned assets; the director wants to stop buying them, so depreciation would shrink, not grow.",null],1],["cdl3",0,1,"When comparing an on-premises data center with Google Cloud, a company counts only server purchase prices. Which costs are they most likely leaving out of the total cost of ownership?",["Power, cooling, floor space, maintenance contracts and staff time","The monthly price of the virtual machines they plan to run in the cloud after the move","The cost of the application licenses they already own","The number of users who will access the application"],0,"Total cost of ownership includes all direct and indirect costs over time: facilities, power and cooling, hardware refresh, maintenance and the people who run it. Hardware price alone understates on-premises cost.","Section 1.2",[null,"The cloud price is the other side of the comparison and is already counted; the question is about on-premises costs that were left out.","Licenses the company already owns are a cost in both scenarios, not the hidden facilities and staff costs of running a data center.","The number of users affects capacity planning but is not itself a cost item in a total cost of ownership calculation."],2],["cdl4",0,1,"A bank keeps its core ledger in its own data center for regulatory reasons but runs its customer analytics and mobile back end in Google Cloud, connected over a private link. Which deployment model is this?",["Multicloud","Public cloud only","Private cloud only","Hybrid cloud"],3,"Hybrid cloud combines on-premises or private infrastructure with a public cloud. Multicloud means using more than one public cloud provider, which this scenario does not describe.","Section 1.2",["Multicloud means using two or more public cloud providers; here there is one public cloud plus the bank's own data center.","The ledger stays in the bank's own data center, so not everything runs in the public cloud.","Part of the workload runs in Google Cloud, a public cloud, so this is not a private-cloud-only setup.",null],1],["cdl5",0,1,"A company uses Google Cloud for analytics and another public cloud provider for its e-commerce site, and has no data center of its own. How is this best described?",["Hybrid cloud","Edge computing","Private cloud","Multicloud"],3,"Using two or more public cloud providers is multicloud. Hybrid would require on-premises or private cloud infrastructure in the mix, and the company has none.","Section 1.2",["Hybrid cloud needs on-premises or private cloud infrastructure, and this company has no data center of its own.","Edge computing means processing data near where it is created, such as in stores or devices, not using two cloud providers.","A private cloud is infrastructure dedicated to one organization; both providers here are public clouds.",null],1],["cdl6",0,1,"A startup wants to run its code without managing operating systems, patches or servers, and only upload the application. Which service model fits best?",["Infrastructure as a service (IaaS)","On-premises virtualization","Platform as a service (PaaS)","Colocation hosting"],2,"PaaS provides the runtime and platform so the customer brings only code and data. IaaS would leave the operating system and patching to the startup.","Section 1.3",["With IaaS the startup would still install, patch and manage the operating system on its VMs.","On-premises virtualization means running and patching your own servers and hypervisors, the opposite of what the startup wants.",null,"Colocation rents space for hardware you own and manage yourself, so servers and patching stay with the startup."],2],["cdl7",0,1,"Which statement about the shared responsibility model is correct?",["Google is responsible for customer data once it is stored in Google Cloud","Customers are always responsible for their data and who can access it","The customer's share of responsibility is the same in IaaS and SaaS","Moving to SaaS makes the customer responsible for the physical data center"],1,"In every service model the customer keeps responsibility for its data, identities and access. The provider's share grows from IaaS to SaaS, so the split is not the same across models.","Section 1.3",["Google secures the infrastructure, but the customer stays responsible for its data and who can access it.",null,"The customer's share is largest in IaaS and smallest in SaaS; it changes with the service model.","Physical data centers are always the provider's responsibility; SaaS moves more work to the provider, not less."],2],["cdl8",0,1,"An organization moves an application from Compute Engine VMs to a fully managed SaaS product. How does its security responsibility change?",["It must now also secure the physical servers","It no longer needs to control who has access to the data","It stays exactly the same as on VMs, because the data and the users have not changed at all","It shrinks, because the provider now manages the platform and the application"],3,"With SaaS the provider manages the infrastructure, platform and application, so the customer's share shrinks, but the customer still owns its data and access management.","Section 1.3",["Physical servers are the provider's job in every cloud model, and even more so with SaaS.","Access to data remains the customer's responsibility in every model, including SaaS.","The split does change: with SaaS the provider takes over the OS, platform and application that the customer managed on VMs.",null],2],["cdl9",0,1,"An application must keep running if one data center building in a region loses power. What is the simplest Google Cloud design choice?",["Run instances in more than one zone of the region","Run all instances in a single zone with larger machines","Store backups on the same VM's disk","Buy a dedicated interconnect to the region"],0,"Zones are isolated deployment areas within a region, so spreading instances across zones survives a single zone failure. Larger machines or local backups in one zone still share its failure.","Section 1.2",[null,"All instances in a single zone share that zone's failure; bigger machines do not help when the building loses power.","Backups on the same VM's disk are lost with the VM and do nothing to keep the application running.","Interconnect improves connectivity from on-premises; it does not protect against a zone failure inside the region."],1],["cdl10",0,1,"Users in Australia complain that a web app hosted only in a US region is slow to respond, even though the servers show low CPU use. What is the most likely cause?",["Not enough bandwidth on the users' home connections, which limits how much data can arrive at once","The app uses too many storage classes","Network latency from the distance between users and the region","The project has too many IAM roles"],2,"Latency is the time for data to travel, and it grows with distance. Serving content from a closer region or edge locations (for example with a CDN) reduces it. Low CPU shows the servers are not the bottleneck.","Section 1.2",["Limited bandwidth slows large downloads, but interactive delay from users on another continent is mainly caused by distance (latency).","Storage classes affect storage price and retrieval cost, not the round-trip time between Australia and the US.",null,"IAM roles control permissions and have no effect on network response time."],2],["cdl11",0,1,"What do Google's edge points of presence mainly do for Google Cloud customers?",["Store customers' primary databases in every city so data never leaves the user's country","Bring traffic onto Google's private network close to users, reducing latency","Replace the need for regions and zones","Provide on-premises hardware installed at customer sites"],1,"Points of presence are where Google's network connects to other networks near users, so traffic rides Google's private backbone for most of its trip and content can be cached at the edge. Regions and zones still host the workloads.","Section 1.2",["Edge locations do not host customers' primary databases; workloads and data live in the regions the customer chooses.",null,"PoPs complement regions and zones; you still run workloads in regions.","PoPs are Google network locations, not hardware installed at customer sites."],2],["cdl12",0,1,"A CIO is worried about being locked in to one provider. Which Google Cloud approach most directly addresses this?",["Signing a multi-year committed use contract so that prices stay fixed for longer periods","Using a single region for all workloads","Storing data only in the Archive storage class","Building on open source and open standards such as Kubernetes"],3,"Open source and open standards (Kubernetes, Apache Beam, TensorFlow, PostgreSQL) let workloads and skills move between environments. Commitments and single-region designs do nothing for portability.","Section 1.1",["A multi-year commitment ties the company more tightly to one provider, which increases rather than reduces lock-in.","Using one region affects availability and latency but does nothing to make workloads portable to another provider.","The Archive storage class is about cost for rarely read data; it does not make data or applications more portable.",null],2],["cdl13",0,1,"A retailer moved its servers to the cloud a year ago, but release cycles, approvals and team structures are unchanged and little has improved. What is most likely missing?",["A larger machine type for each server so the migrated applications run faster than before","A second cloud provider","More storage capacity","Cultural and process change alongside the technology"],3,"Digital transformation is about changing how people and processes work, not only where servers run. Without new ways of working (small empowered teams, automation, experimentation) the benefits stay small.","Section 1.1",["Bigger machines make servers faster but leave the slow approvals and team structures, the real problem, unchanged.","A second cloud provider adds complexity without changing how the retailer's people and processes work.","Storage capacity is not the constraint; release cycles and approvals are process and culture issues.",null],2],["cdl14",0,1,"Which scenario is the best example of SaaS?",["A company's staff use Google Workspace for email and documents","A developer deploys code to App Engine","An administrator creates Compute Engine VMs and installs a database","A team runs containers on a GKE cluster it configures"],0,"SaaS is a complete application consumed by users, such as Google Workspace. App Engine is PaaS, Compute Engine is IaaS, and GKE is a managed container platform where the team still configures workloads.","Section 1.3",[null,"App Engine is platform as a service: the developer still writes and deploys the application code.","Creating VMs and installing a database is infrastructure as a service, where the customer manages the OS and software.","Configuring and running containers on a GKE cluster is a managed platform, not a finished application consumed by users."],1],["cdl15",0,1,"A company must launch a pilot product in a new country within weeks and may cancel it after three months. Why is the cloud a good fit?",["Cloud resources can only be bought in three-year terms","It removes all compliance obligations in the new country because the provider handles them for customers","Resources can be provisioned quickly in nearby regions and released if the pilot ends","It guarantees the product will succeed"],2,"Agility and pay-as-you-go let teams experiment quickly without long-term hardware commitments, and global regions put the pilot close to users. Compliance obligations still apply in the new country.","Section 1.1",["Cloud resources are normally pay-as-you-go with no term; commitments are optional discounts, not a requirement.","Compliance obligations in the new country still apply; the provider helps with its share, but the customer remains responsible for its own.",null,"No platform guarantees business success; the cloud only makes it cheaper and faster to test the idea."],2],["cdl16",0,2,"A media company stores millions of photos, videos and PDF contracts with no fixed schema. What type of data is this?",["Structured data","Semi-structured data","Relational data","Unstructured data"],3,"Images, video and documents have no predefined data model, so they are unstructured data, usually kept in object storage such as Cloud Storage. JSON with tags and keys would be semi-structured.","Section 2.1",["Structured data fits fixed rows and columns, which photos, videos and PDFs do not.","Semi-structured data has tags or keys, like JSON; media files and scanned contracts have no such internal structure.","Relational data is structured data in related tables, the opposite of files with no fixed schema.",null],1],["cdl17",0,2,"A company must keep backup files for seven years to satisfy auditors and expects to read them less than once a year. Which Cloud Storage class is the most cost-effective?",["Standard","Nearline","Archive","Coldline"],2,"Archive has the lowest storage price and suits data read less than once a year, with a 365-day minimum storage duration. Coldline suits roughly quarterly access and Nearline monthly access, at higher storage prices.","Section 2.2",["Standard has the highest storage price and is meant for frequently accessed data, not backups read less than once a year.","Nearline suits data read about once a month and costs more to store than Archive for seven-year retention.",null,"Coldline suits roughly quarterly access; Archive is cheaper for data read less than once a year."],1],["cdl18",0,2,"A web team serves product images to customers all day, every day, from Cloud Storage. Which storage class fits?",["Standard","Archive","Coldline","Nearline"],0,"Standard is for frequently accessed (hot) data and has no minimum storage duration or retrieval fees. The colder classes lower the storage price but add retrieval costs that would be expensive for constant reads.","Section 2.2",[null,"Archive has a 365-day minimum and retrieval fees on every read, so constant image serving would be very expensive.","Coldline charges retrieval fees on every read and is meant for data read about once a quarter.","Nearline adds retrieval fees and is designed for data read about once a month, not all day."],1],["cdl19",0,2,"An existing application uses a PostgreSQL database that the company wants to stop patching and backing up by hand, with as few code changes as possible. What should it use?",["Bigtable","Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL","Firestore","BigQuery, loading the tables into a data warehouse"],1,"Cloud SQL is a managed relational database service for MySQL, PostgreSQL and SQL Server that handles patching, backups and replication. Bigtable and Firestore are NoSQL and would need a rewrite; BigQuery is an analytics warehouse.","Section 2.2",["Bigtable is a wide-column NoSQL database; moving a PostgreSQL app to it would require a rewrite.",null,"Firestore is a NoSQL document database, so the relational PostgreSQL schema and queries would have to be redesigned.","BigQuery is an analytics warehouse, not a transactional database for an application's day-to-day reads and writes."],2],["cdl20",0,2,"A global payments company needs a relational database with strong consistency that scales horizontally and serves users on several continents. Which service fits best?",["Cloud SQL","Firestore","Spanner","Cloud Storage"],2,"Spanner is a globally distributed relational database with strong consistency and horizontal scaling. Cloud SQL is regional and scales mainly vertically; Firestore is a document database.","Section 2.2",["Cloud SQL is a regional database that scales mainly vertically, so it does not fit global horizontal scale.","Firestore is a NoSQL document database, not a relational database with SQL transactions across continents.",null,"Cloud Storage is object storage for files, not a relational database with transactions."],3],["cdl21",0,2,"An IoT company ingests billions of sensor readings per day and needs very fast reads and writes of time-series data by device and timestamp. Which database fits best?",["Bigtable","Cloud SQL","Looker","AlloyDB"],0,"Bigtable is a wide-column NoSQL database built for huge volumes of low-latency, high-throughput data such as time series and IoT. Cloud SQL and AlloyDB are relational databases, and Looker is a BI tool.","Section 2.2",[null,"Cloud SQL is a relational database on a single primary instance and is not built for billions of writes per day.","Looker is a business intelligence tool for reports and dashboards; it does not store sensor data.","AlloyDB is a high-performance PostgreSQL database, but massive time-series ingestion by key is Bigtable's specialty."],2],["cdl22",0,2,"A mobile game needs a serverless database that syncs player data to devices in real time and still works when a phone goes offline. Which service fits best?",["Bigtable","BigQuery","Cloud SQL for MySQL","Firestore"],3,"Firestore is a serverless NoSQL document database with real-time listeners and offline support for mobile and web clients. BigQuery is for analytics and Bigtable targets massive analytical and operational throughput, not client sync.","Section 2.2",["Bigtable targets huge analytical and operational throughput and has no built-in real-time sync to mobile devices or offline mode.","BigQuery is an analytics warehouse, not an app database that syncs data to player devices.","Cloud SQL for MySQL is relational and server-based, with no built-in real-time client listeners or offline support.",null],3],["cdl23",0,2,"Analysts want to run SQL across several terabytes of sales history from many systems without managing any servers or indexes. What should they use?",["Cloud SQL","Memorystore","Firestore","BigQuery"],3,"BigQuery is a serverless data warehouse that scans large datasets with SQL and separates storage from compute. Cloud SQL is an operational database for applications and is not designed for this scale of analytics.","Section 2.2",["Cloud SQL runs application transactions and is not designed to scan terabytes of history from many systems.","Memorystore is an in-memory cache (Redis or Memcached), not a SQL data warehouse.","Firestore is a document database for apps and does not support large-scale SQL analytics.",null],1],["cdl24",0,2,"A logistics company wants delivery-truck GPS events analyzed within seconds and stored for dashboards. Which pair of services is the typical pipeline?",["Pub/Sub to ingest events and Dataflow to process them into BigQuery","Transfer Appliance to ingest and Looker Studio to process","Cloud Storage Archive to ingest the events and Cloud SQL to process and aggregate them every night","Cloud Run functions to ingest and Nearline to process"],0,"Pub/Sub ingests streaming events and delivers them asynchronously; Dataflow transforms them in real time and writes to BigQuery. Transfer Appliance is for bulk offline transfer, and storage classes do not process data.","Section 2.3",[null,"Transfer Appliance is a physical device for bulk offline transfer, and Looker Studio visualizes data rather than processing streams.","Archive storage is for rarely read data and a nightly Cloud SQL job cannot analyze events within seconds.","Nearline is a storage class that cannot process data, and it is meant for data read about once a month."],2],["cdl25",0,2,"A company has many existing Apache Spark and Hadoop jobs and wants to run them in Google Cloud with minimal changes. Which service fits?",["Dataflow","Pub/Sub","Dataproc","Looker"],2,"Dataproc is managed Spark and Hadoop, so existing jobs move with little change. Dataflow runs Apache Beam pipelines, which would mean rewriting the jobs.","Section 2.3",["Dataflow runs Apache Beam pipelines, so existing Spark and Hadoop jobs would need to be rewritten.","Pub/Sub is a messaging service that moves events; it cannot run Spark or Hadoop jobs.",null,"Looker is a BI tool for exploring data; it does not execute Spark or Hadoop processing."],2],["cdl26",0,2,"Different departments report different revenue numbers because each defines 'revenue' in its own spreadsheet. Which Google Cloud capability best addresses this?",["Looker with a governed semantic model so metrics are defined once","Moving every spreadsheet to Coldline storage","Creating a separate BigQuery dataset for each department so every team can keep its own definitions","Giving every analyst the Owner role"],0,"Looker's semantic layer (LookML) defines business metrics once so every report uses the same definition. Separate datasets or broader permissions would make the inconsistency worse.","Section 2.3",[null,"Moving spreadsheets to Coldline only changes where the files are stored, not how revenue is defined.","Separate datasets with separate definitions would lock in the inconsistency instead of fixing it.","The Owner role grants broad permissions and does nothing to make metric definitions consistent."],3],["cdl27",0,2,"A company must move 400 TB from its data center to Cloud Storage but has a slow internet connection that would take months. What is the best option?",["Storage Transfer Service over the existing connection","Database Migration Service","BigQuery Data Transfer Service","Transfer Appliance"],3,"Transfer Appliance is a physical device shipped to the customer, filled with data and shipped back to Google, which avoids months of network transfer. The other services all move data over the network.","Section 2.2",["Storage Transfer Service moves data over the network, which the scenario says would take months on the slow connection.","Database Migration Service migrates databases to Cloud SQL or AlloyDB, not 400 TB of files to Cloud Storage.","BigQuery Data Transfer Service schedules loads into BigQuery from SaaS and other sources, not bulk file transfer from a data center.",null],2],["cdl28",0,2,"A marketing team wants data from Google Ads and other SaaS sources loaded into BigQuery on a regular schedule without writing code. What should they use?",["Storage Transfer Service, copying exported files from each source into Cloud Storage","BigQuery Data Transfer Service","Transfer Appliance","Database Migration Service"],1,"BigQuery Data Transfer Service schedules managed loads from supported SaaS applications and other sources into BigQuery. Database Migration Service moves databases into Cloud SQL or AlloyDB.","Section 2.2",["Storage Transfer Service copies files into Cloud Storage; it does not load Google Ads data into BigQuery tables on a schedule.",null,"Transfer Appliance is a physical device for one-off bulk transfers, not scheduled loads from SaaS sources.","Database Migration Service moves operational databases into Cloud SQL or AlloyDB, not SaaS data into BigQuery."],2],["cdl29",0,2,"A healthcare company wants analysts to find trusted datasets, see who owns them and ensure only approved people can view sensitive columns. Which practice is this?",["Data governance","Data archiving","Streaming analytics","Load balancing"],0,"Data governance covers discovering and cataloging data, ownership, quality, and access policies such as restricting sensitive fields. Archiving and streaming are about storage and movement, not control.","Section 2.1",[null,"Archiving moves rarely used data to cheaper storage; it does not help people find trusted data or restrict sensitive columns.","Streaming analytics processes events in real time; it does not cover ownership, cataloging or access policy.","Load balancing spreads network traffic across servers and has nothing to do with managing data."],2],["cdl30",0,3,"Which statement best describes machine learning?",["A set of if-then rules written by programmers in advance to handle every possible case the system meets","A system that learns patterns from data to make predictions or decisions","Any software that stores data in the cloud","A type of database optimized for analytics"],1,"Machine learning systems learn patterns from example data instead of being explicitly programmed with rules. Hand-written rules are traditional programming, even when they are complex.","Section 3.1",["Hand-written if-then rules are traditional programming; ML learns patterns from data instead.",null,"Storing data in the cloud is not machine learning; ML is about learning from data to predict or decide.","A database optimized for analytics is a data warehouse such as BigQuery, not machine learning."],1],["cdl31",0,3,"A marketing team wants a tool that writes first drafts of product descriptions from a few bullet points. What kind of AI is this?",["Predictive analytics","Rule-based automation","Generative AI","Optical character recognition"],2,"Generative AI creates new content such as text, images or code. Predictive models forecast or classify from existing data, and OCR only reads text from images.","Section 3.1",["Predictive analytics forecasts or classifies from existing data; it does not write new product descriptions.","Rule-based automation follows fixed logic and cannot compose varied new text from a few bullet points.",null,"OCR reads existing text from images; it does not create new content."],1],["cdl32",0,3,"A company calculates sales tax with a fixed formula set by law. A manager suggests using ML for it. What is the best response?",["ML is needed because tax is a numerical problem","ML should be used so the formula can adjust itself automatically whenever tax laws change in the future","A pre-trained Vision API should be used","ML is a poor fit because the rules are known and exact, so normal code is better"],3,"ML shines when patterns are too complex to write as rules. When exact, stable rules exist, conventional code is cheaper, explainable and always correct; a model could only approximate them.","Section 3.1",["Being numerical does not make a problem suited to ML; a known formula is computed exactly by ordinary code.","Tax law changes are published rules that developers update in code; a model adjusting itself would only approximate the law.","The Vision API analyzes images and has nothing to do with calculating tax.",null],2],["cdl33",0,3,"A loan-approval model trained mostly on data from one city performs poorly for applicants from rural areas. What data quality problem does this show?",["The training data was not representative of the people the model serves","The model was stored in the wrong region","The data was encrypted at rest","There was too little compute available during training, so the model could not finish learning the patterns"],0,"When training data does not represent the full population, the model can be inaccurate or unfair for under-represented groups. Encryption and region choice do not affect what the model learned.","Section 3.1",[null,"The storage region of a model does not change what it learned or how accurate it is for rural applicants.","Encryption at rest protects data confidentiality but has no effect on what the model learns.","More compute speeds up training, but it cannot teach patterns that are missing from unrepresentative data."],2],["cdl34",0,3,"A company deploying an AI model for hiring decisions wants to be able to tell candidates why the model made a recommendation. Which responsible AI practice is this?",["Data residency","Data compression","Autoscaling","Explainability"],3,"Explainability means being able to describe which factors drove a prediction, which supports fairness reviews and accountability. It is one of the practices behind Google's AI Principles.","Section 3.1",["Data residency is about where data is stored, not about explaining a model's recommendation.","Data compression reduces storage size and has nothing to do with explaining predictions.","Autoscaling changes capacity with load and does not help explain why a model made a decision.",null],2],["cdl35",0,3,"A retailer wants to automatically read text from photos of printed receipts that customers upload, and has no ML team. What is the fastest option?",["Train a custom model from scratch with Vertex AI custom training on labeled receipts","Build a Spanner database","Call the pre-trained Cloud Vision API text detection","Use BigQuery ML linear regression"],2,"The pre-trained Vision API already detects text in images and needs no training data or ML expertise. Custom training would take far longer and a regression model does not read images.","Section 3.2",["Custom training from scratch needs ML expertise, lots of labeled data and time, while a pre-trained API already reads text.","Spanner is a relational database; it stores data but cannot read text from images.",null,"Linear regression predicts numbers from tabular data and cannot read text from a photo."],2],["cdl36",0,3,"A support center wants to translate customer chat messages between 20 languages in real time. Which Google Cloud option fits with the least effort?",["The Cloud Translation API","AutoML with a custom dataset","A Bigtable instance","Training a model on TPUs"],0,"The Translation API is a pre-trained service for translating text between many languages. Training your own translation model is only worth it for highly specialized vocabulary.","Section 3.2",[null,"AutoML needs a custom labeled dataset and training, which is unnecessary when a pre-trained translation service exists.","Bigtable is a NoSQL database and does not translate anything.","Training a translation model on TPUs is a large custom project, the most effort rather than the least."],1],["cdl37",0,3,"Data analysts who know SQL but not Python want to forecast next quarter's sales from data already in BigQuery. What is the best option?",["Export the data to spreadsheets and hire a data science team to build a model in Python","Use BigQuery ML to create and run the model with SQL","Use the Cloud Vision API","Move the data to Firestore first"],1,"BigQuery ML lets analysts create, train and use models with SQL where the data already lives, with no data movement. The Vision API is for images.","Section 3.2",["Exporting data and hiring a team adds time and cost, when the analysts can build the model in BigQuery with SQL.",null,"The Vision API analyzes images, not sales tables.","Firestore is a document database for apps and would move data away from where it can be analyzed and modeled."],2],["cdl38",0,3,"A manufacturer wants to detect defects specific to its own products in assembly-line images. It has thousands of labeled images but no ML experts. What is the best fit?",["The pre-trained Natural Language API","Custom training with hand-written TensorFlow code","A Cloud SQL database","AutoML image training in Vertex AI"],3,"AutoML trains a custom model on your own labeled data with little ML expertise. Pre-trained APIs do not know these specific defects, and hand-written custom training needs data scientists.","Section 3.2",["The Natural Language API analyzes text, not images of product defects.","Hand-written custom training needs ML experts, which the manufacturer does not have.","Cloud SQL could store results, but a database cannot learn to recognize defects in images.",null],3],["cdl39",0,3,"A company's data science team has a unique recommendation algorithm that is a key competitive advantage and needs full control over the model code. What should it use?",["A pre-trained API","Only BigQuery ML's built-in models","Looker Studio","Vertex AI custom training"],3,"Custom training on Vertex AI gives full control of the code, framework and model for differentiated problems, at the cost of more expertise and time. Pre-trained APIs cannot be customized to that degree.","Section 3.2",["A pre-trained API solves common tasks and cannot be controlled at the code level for a unique, differentiating algorithm.","BigQuery ML's built-in model types are convenient but do not give full control over custom model code.","Looker Studio builds dashboards and reports; it does not train models.",null],3],["cdl40",0,3,"A team wants to compare several foundation models, including Google's and open models, before choosing one to build on in Vertex AI. Where should it look?",["Vertex AI Model Garden","Cloud Storage Nearline","The Cloud Billing console","Security Command Center"],0,"Model Garden is the catalog in Vertex AI for discovering, testing and deploying Google, partner and open models. The other options are storage, billing and security tools.","Section 3.2",[null,"Nearline is a Cloud Storage class for infrequently accessed data, not a catalog of models.","The billing console shows costs; it does not let you discover or compare foundation models.","Security Command Center reports security risks and threats, not AI models."],3],["cdl41",0,3,"A company's internal chatbot built on a large language model sometimes gives confident but made-up answers about HR policies. What approach best reduces this?",["Increase the number of GPUs serving the model so that it has more capacity to think through each answer","Store the chatbot logs in Archive storage","Ground the model's answers in the company's approved HR documents","Remove all users' access to the chatbot"],2,"Grounding connects the model to trusted, current sources such as company documents, so answers are based on real content and hallucinations are reduced. More hardware does not make answers more accurate.","Section 3.2",["More GPUs make responses faster or allow more users but do not make the model's facts correct.","Changing where logs are stored does nothing to the accuracy of the chatbot's answers.",null,"Removing all access avoids wrong answers only by abandoning the tool, rather than fixing its accuracy."],3],["cdl42",0,3,"What are Tensor Processing Units (TPUs)?",["Google-designed chips that accelerate machine learning workloads","A type of Cloud Storage bucket for training data","A pricing discount for AI services","Google's managed Kubernetes nodes that run web applications and scale them automatically on demand"],0,"TPUs are custom accelerators (ASICs) that Google designed for machine learning training and inference. They are offered in Google Cloud alongside GPUs.","Section 3.2",[null,"TPUs are processors, not storage buckets; training data would live in Cloud Storage or BigQuery.","TPUs are hardware accelerators, not a pricing or discount program.","GKE nodes are VMs for running containers; TPUs are specialized ML chips that can be attached to some workloads."],1],["cdl43",0,3,"A business leader asks which factor matters most when choosing between a pre-trained API, AutoML and custom training. Which answer is best?",["The color scheme of the console","The number of IAM roles in the project","Which region has the most zones, since AI services must run in the largest region available to be effective","How unique the problem is, and the time, data and ML skills available"],3,"The trade-off is effort versus differentiation: pre-trained APIs are fastest for common tasks, AutoML suits custom data with little expertise, and custom training suits unique problems when skilled people and time are available.","Section 3.2",["The console's appearance has no bearing on which AI approach suits a business problem.","The number of IAM roles is a security matter, not a factor in choosing between pre-trained, AutoML and custom models.","AI services do not need the region with the most zones; region choice is about latency, residency and availability.",null],3],["cdl44",0,4,"A company must leave its data center in three months when the lease ends and has no time to change its applications. Which migration approach fits?",["Refactor the applications into microservices running on GKE before the lease ends","Reimagine the business process","Retire every application","Rehost (lift and shift) onto Compute Engine VMs"],3,"Rehosting moves applications as they are, which is the fastest way to exit a data center on a deadline. Refactoring and reimagining deliver more cloud value but take far longer.","Section 4.1",["Refactoring into microservices takes far longer than three months and the company has no time to change applications.","Reimagining the business process is the most time-consuming approach and does not fit a hard deadline.","Retiring every application would shut down systems the business still needs.",null],1],["cdl45",0,4,"During a migration assessment, a team finds a reporting tool that nobody has used for two years. What is the best migration decision for it?",["Rehost it","Refactor it","Retire it","Replatform it"],2,"Applications that are no longer used should be retired rather than migrated, which reduces cost and effort. Moving an unused tool in any form wastes money.","Section 4.1",["Rehosting an unused tool spends migration effort and ongoing cloud cost on something nobody uses.","Refactoring takes the most effort of all, which makes no sense for an unused application.",null,"Replatforming still moves and runs the tool in the cloud, which wastes money when nobody uses it."],1],["cdl46",0,4,"A team moves a web application to Compute Engine but switches its self-managed MySQL database to Cloud SQL, without rewriting the application. Which approach is this?",["Rehost","Retain","Refactor into microservices","Replatform"],3,"Replatforming (move and improve) makes targeted changes, such as adopting a managed database, to gain some cloud benefits without re-architecting. A pure rehost would keep the self-managed database.","Section 4.1",["A pure rehost would keep the self-managed MySQL database; adopting Cloud SQL is a targeted improvement.","Retain means leaving the application where it is, but this one is moving to Compute Engine.","Refactoring changes the code and architecture, while this application's code is not being rewritten.",null],2],["cdl47",0,4,"A web tier on Compute Engine must add VMs automatically when CPU load rises and replace any VM that fails its health check. What should the team use?",["A single large VM with a bigger disk","A managed instance group with autoscaling and autohealing","A Cloud Storage bucket","A committed use discount on the existing VMs so extra capacity costs less during peaks"],1,"Managed instance groups create identical VMs from a template, autoscale on signals such as CPU, and recreate unhealthy instances. A single VM can neither scale out nor heal itself, and discounts change price, not behavior.","Section 4.2",["A single VM cannot add instances or replace itself when it fails, however large its disk.",null,"A Cloud Storage bucket stores objects; it does not run or scale a web tier.","A committed use discount lowers the price of VMs but does not create or heal instances automatically."],2],["cdl48",0,4,"A company runs hundreds of VMware virtual machines on-premises and wants to move them to Google Cloud quickly while keeping its VMware tools and processes. Which service fits?",["Google Cloud VMware Engine","Cloud Run with each VM repackaged as a container","App Engine","Firestore"],0,"Google Cloud VMware Engine runs a native VMware environment in Google Cloud, so existing VMs, tools and skills carry over. Cloud Run and App Engine would require repackaging the applications.","Section 4.2",[null,"Repackaging hundreds of VMs as containers is a large rework, not a quick move that keeps VMware tools.","App Engine runs application code on a managed platform and cannot host existing VMware virtual machines.","Firestore is a document database and cannot run virtual machines."],2],["cdl49",0,4,"A company runs a specialized database that must stay on dedicated physical servers for licensing reasons, but it wants low-latency access from Google Cloud services. Which option fits?",["Cloud Run functions","Spot VMs","Bare Metal Solution","Cloud Storage Coldline"],2,"Bare Metal Solution provides dedicated physical servers close to Google Cloud regions for specialized workloads such as some Oracle databases. Serverless and Spot VMs are shared, virtualized options.","Section 4.2",["Cloud Run functions runs short event-driven code on shared serverless infrastructure, not a database on dedicated physical servers.","Spot VMs are shared, interruptible virtual machines, the opposite of dedicated physical servers for a critical database.",null,"Coldline is a storage class for rarely read data and cannot run a database."],3],["cdl50",0,4,"What is the main reason containers make applications portable?",["They package the application with its dependencies so it runs the same in any environment","They include a full guest operating system for every application, so nothing depends on the host machine at all","They can only run on one cloud provider","They remove the need for any code changes when scaling a database"],0,"A container bundles the code, runtime and libraries it needs and shares the host's kernel, so it runs consistently on a laptop, on-premises or in any cloud. Containers are lighter than VMs because they do not carry a full guest OS.","Section 4.2",[null,"It is VMs that carry a full guest OS; containers share the host kernel, which is why they are lighter.","Containers run on any platform with a container runtime; portability across providers is their main benefit.","Containers package applications; they do not remove code changes needed to scale a database."],1],["cdl51",0,4,"A company wants to run Kubernetes but does not want to manage, size or patch worker nodes, and wants to pay for the resources its pods request. Which option fits?",["GKE Standard mode","GKE Autopilot mode","Self-managed Kubernetes on Compute Engine","Bare Metal Solution"],1,"In Autopilot, Google manages the nodes and you pay for the resources pods request. In Standard mode the customer manages and pays for the node pools, and self-managed Kubernetes adds even more work.","Section 4.2",["In Standard mode the customer manages the node pools and pays for the node VMs, which the company wants to avoid.",null,"Self-managed Kubernetes means running the control plane and nodes yourself, the most operational work of all.","Bare Metal Solution provides dedicated physical servers and is not a managed Kubernetes service."],3],["cdl52",0,4,"A developer has a stateless containerized API that gets no traffic at night and bursts during the day. The team wants no infrastructure to manage and no cost when idle. Which service fits best?",["Compute Engine with a fixed number of VMs","GKE Standard with three always-on nodes","Cloud Run","Bare Metal Solution"],2,"Cloud Run runs containers serverlessly, scales automatically with requests and can scale to zero so idle time costs nothing. Fixed VMs and always-on nodes keep charging overnight.","Section 4.3",["A fixed number of VMs keeps running and billing overnight and needs infrastructure management.","Always-on GKE nodes charge even with no traffic and still need cluster management.",null,"Bare Metal Solution is dedicated physical hardware for specialized workloads, with ongoing cost and management."],2],["cdl53",0,4,"A small piece of code must run each time a file is uploaded to a Cloud Storage bucket, to create a thumbnail. Which option is the simplest fit?",["A managed instance group","An event-driven function with Cloud Run functions","A GKE cluster","Google Cloud VMware Engine"],1,"Cloud Run functions (formerly Cloud Functions) runs short single-purpose code in response to events such as a new object in a bucket, with no servers to manage. The other options need far more setup for one small task.","Section 4.3",["A managed instance group keeps VMs running and needs setup, far more than a small thumbnail task requires.",null,"A GKE cluster is a full container platform, heavy for one small event-triggered function.","VMware Engine runs whole VMware environments and is not meant for small event-driven code."],2],["cdl54",0,4,"A company has a large monolithic application where a small change requires redeploying everything and one bug can bring down the whole system. What modernization is usually proposed?",["Moving it to a larger single VM","Breaking it into microservices that are deployed and scaled independently","Storing its logs in Archive storage","Removing its load balancer"],1,"Microservices split an application into small services with their own deployments, so teams release independently and failures are isolated. A bigger VM keeps the same coupling.","Section 4.1",["A larger VM keeps the same single deployment unit, so small changes still redeploy everything and one bug still affects all of it.",null,"Where logs are stored does not change how the application is deployed or how failures spread.","Removing the load balancer reduces resilience and does nothing to decouple the application."],1],["cdl55",0,4,"A bank wants to let fintech partners use its account-balance service securely, with rate limits, API keys, usage analytics and a developer portal. Which Google Cloud product fits?",["Cloud Armor","Pub/Sub","Apigee","Cloud CDN"],2,"Apigee is Google Cloud's API management platform: security, quotas and rate limiting, analytics, versioning and developer portals. Cloud Armor protects against web attacks but does not manage APIs as products.","Section 4.4",["Cloud Armor protects web apps from DDoS and attacks, but it does not provide API keys, developer portals or usage analytics as products.","Pub/Sub moves messages between systems; it does not manage partner access, rate limits or portals for APIs.",null,"Cloud CDN caches content at the edge and has no API management features."],3],["cdl56",0,4,"A company runs Kubernetes clusters in its own data centers, in Google Cloud and in another cloud, and wants to manage configuration and security policy for all of them consistently. Which option fits?",["GKE Enterprise","Cloud SQL","App Engine","Compute Engine sole-tenant nodes"],0,"GKE Enterprise (formerly Anthos) manages fleets of Kubernetes clusters across Google Cloud, on-premises and other clouds with consistent configuration and policy. The other options run in Google Cloud only.","Section 4.5",[null,"Cloud SQL is a managed relational database and does not manage Kubernetes clusters.","App Engine is a platform for web apps inside Google Cloud and does not manage clusters in other environments.","Sole-tenant nodes give dedicated hosts in Google Cloud only and do not manage clusters elsewhere."],3],["cdl57",0,4,"A company runs a nightly batch job that can be interrupted and restarted without harm and wants the lowest possible VM price. Which Compute Engine option fits?",["Sole-tenant nodes","Spot VMs","Custom machine types with extra memory","Always-on standard VMs with no discounts"],1,"Spot VMs offer large discounts on spare capacity but can be stopped at any time, which suits fault-tolerant batch work. Sole-tenant nodes cost more because they reserve physical hosts.","Section 4.2",["Sole-tenant nodes reserve whole physical hosts and cost more, not less.",null,"Custom machine types match size to need but are priced like standard VMs, without the large spare-capacity discount.","Standard VMs with no discounts are the most expensive option for an interruptible job."],2],["cdl58",0,4,"An application needs a specific older operating system and full control over installed software and kernel settings. Which compute option fits best?",["Cloud Run","App Engine standard environment","Cloud Run functions","Compute Engine virtual machines"],3,"Compute Engine VMs give full control over the OS and software, which legacy or specialized workloads need. Serverless platforms manage the OS for you and restrict what you can change.","Section 4.2",["Cloud Run manages the operating system for you, so you cannot choose an older OS or change kernel settings.","App Engine standard provides managed runtimes and does not allow control over the OS or kernel.","Cloud Run functions runs code on a managed runtime with no access to the underlying operating system.",null],2],["cdl59",0,5,"An attacker changes the amounts in stored payment records without anyone noticing. Which security property was violated?",["Confidentiality","Availability","Integrity","Privacy"],2,"Integrity means data is accurate and has not been altered without authorization. Confidentiality would be violated if the records were read by someone unauthorized, and availability if they could not be reached.","Section 5.1",["Confidentiality is broken when data is seen by unauthorized people; here the data was changed.","Availability is about being able to reach systems and data; the records were still available, just altered.",null,"Privacy concerns how personal data is collected and used; the core failure here is unauthorized modification."],1],["cdl60",0,5,"A storage bucket holding customer files was made publicly readable by mistake. Under the shared responsibility model, whose responsibility was this setting?",["Google's, because Google runs the storage service and is therefore responsible for all of its settings","The customer's, because it controls configuration and access to its data","The internet service provider's","No one's, because public access cannot be restricted"],1,"Customers are responsible for configuring access to their data; Google secures the underlying service. Misconfiguration by customers is one of the most common causes of cloud data exposure.","Section 5.1",["Google secures the storage service itself, but access settings on the customer's bucket are the customer's responsibility.",null,"The internet provider carries traffic and has no control over bucket permissions.","Public access can be restricted with IAM and organization policies such as public access prevention."],1],["cdl61",0,5,"A finance employee receives an email that looks like it is from the CEO, asking them to sign in through a link to approve an urgent payment. What threat is this and what control helps most?",["Phishing; phishing-resistant two-step verification such as security keys","Ransomware; a larger VM","A DDoS attack; the Archive storage class","Misconfiguration; a committed use discount"],0,"This is phishing that tries to steal credentials. Security keys and other phishing-resistant 2SV stop stolen passwords alone from granting access, and user awareness training helps people spot the lure.","Section 5.1",[null,"The email is a credential-stealing lure, not ransomware, and a larger VM does nothing against it.","A DDoS attack floods a service with traffic; a storage class is not a defense against anything here.","This is a social-engineering attack, not a configuration mistake, and a pricing discount is not a security control."],1],["cdl62",0,5,"Which approach best describes zero trust?",["Trust every device on the corporate network","Block all remote workers","Verify every access request using identity, device and context, regardless of network location","Rely on one strong perimeter firewall"],2,"Zero trust assumes no implicit trust from network location. Each request is checked with identity, device state and context and granted least privilege. Google's BeyondCorp is its implementation of this model.","Section 5.1",["Trusting every device on the network is the old perimeter model that zero trust replaces.","Zero trust enables secure remote work by verifying each request; it does not block remote workers.",null,"A single perimeter firewall is the castle-and-moat model; zero trust removes trust based on network location."],2],["cdl63",0,5,"A security team uses IAM, firewall rules, encryption, logging and a web application firewall together, so that if one control fails others still protect the data. What is this approach called?",["Single sign-on","Defense in depth","Data residency","Lift and shift"],1,"Defense in depth layers several independent controls so one failure does not expose the system. Single sign-on is only one identity control.","Section 5.1",["Single sign-on is one identity control, not a strategy of layering many independent controls.",null,"Data residency is about where data is stored, not layering security controls.","Lift and shift is a migration approach, not a security principle."],1],["cdl64",0,5,"How is customer data stored in Google Cloud protected at rest by default?",["It is not encrypted unless the customer turns on encryption for each bucket, disk and database","It is encrypted only for Archive storage","It is encrypted by default with Google-managed keys, with no action needed","It is encrypted only if the customer uploads its own keys"],2,"Google Cloud encrypts all customer data at rest by default using Google-managed keys. Customers can choose customer-managed keys in Cloud KMS for more control, but encryption does not depend on it.","Section 5.2",["Encryption at rest is on by default in Google Cloud; customers do not need to turn it on per resource.","All customer data is encrypted at rest by default, whatever the storage class.",null,"Customer-supplied or managed keys are optional; data is encrypted by default even without them."],2],["cdl65",0,5,"A regulator requires a company to control when its encryption keys are rotated and to be able to disable them itself. What should it use?",["Customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK) in Cloud KMS","Default Google-managed encryption only","Coldline storage","A larger number of IAM basic roles"],0,"CMEK lets the customer create, rotate, disable and destroy the keys that protect its data in supported services. Default encryption is automatic but managed entirely by Google.","Section 5.2",[null,"Default Google-managed encryption protects data, but Google controls rotation, so the company cannot disable the keys itself.","Coldline is a storage class for rarely read data and gives no control over encryption keys.","Basic IAM roles are broad permissions and have nothing to do with key rotation; they also weaken least privilege."],3],["cdl66",0,5,"A contractor needs to view Compute Engine instances in one project but must not change anything. What is the best practice?",["Grant the Owner role on the organization","Grant the Editor role on the project so the contractor does not need to ask for more access later","Share an administrator's password","Grant a view-only Compute role on that project only"],3,"Least privilege means the narrowest role on the smallest scope that still lets the person do the job. Owner or Editor would allow changes, and sharing passwords removes accountability.","Section 5.2",["Owner on the organization gives full control of everything, far beyond viewing instances in one project.","Editor allows changing resources, which the contractor must not do, and granting extra access in advance breaks least privilege.","Sharing an administrator's password gives full admin rights and removes accountability for who did what.",null],2],["cdl67",0,5,"Which Google Cloud IAM statement is correct?",["Roles are granted to principals, and each role is a collection of permissions","Permissions are granted directly to users one at a time","Basic roles such as Editor are recommended for production least privilege","IAM applies only to Compute Engine"],0,"In IAM you grant roles, which bundle permissions, to principals such as users, groups and service accounts on a resource. Basic roles are broad and are discouraged for production.","Section 5.2",[null,"In IAM you grant roles, which bundle permissions; permissions are not granted to users one by one.","Basic roles are broad and discouraged in production; predefined or custom roles support least privilege.","IAM controls access across almost all Google Cloud services, not just Compute Engine."],2],["cdl68",0,5,"A public website is hit by a large distributed denial-of-service attack and by attempts at SQL injection. Which Google Cloud service helps defend against both at the edge?",["Cloud KMS","Cloud Armor","VPC Service Controls","Assured Workloads"],1,"Cloud Armor provides DDoS protection and web application firewall rules for applications behind Google Cloud's external load balancers. Cloud KMS manages keys and VPC Service Controls limits data exfiltration from services.","Section 5.2",["Cloud KMS manages encryption keys and does not filter web attacks or absorb DDoS traffic.",null,"VPC Service Controls limits data exfiltration from managed services; it does not protect public websites from DDoS.","Assured Workloads applies compliance controls such as data location; it does not block web attacks."],2],["cdl69",0,5,"A company wants to reduce the risk that data in BigQuery and Cloud Storage is copied out to projects outside the company, even by someone with valid credentials. What should it use?",["Cloud CDN","Spot VMs","VPC Service Controls","Looker Studio"],2,"VPC Service Controls creates service perimeters around Google Cloud services to reduce data exfiltration, even when credentials are stolen. The other options are not security controls.","Section 5.2",["Cloud CDN caches content at the edge and does not stop data being copied out of BigQuery.","Spot VMs are discounted, interruptible VMs, not a data protection control.",null,"Looker Studio builds dashboards and can itself be used to view data; it does not prevent exfiltration."],3],["cdl70",0,5,"A security team wants one place in Google Cloud to see misconfigurations, vulnerabilities and threats across all its projects. What should it use?",["Security Command Center","Cloud Billing reports","Database Migration Service","Vertex AI Studio"],0,"Security Command Center gives a centralized view of security posture, vulnerabilities, misconfigurations and threats across an organization. Billing reports show costs, not risks.","Section 5.2",[null,"Billing reports show spending, not misconfigurations or threats.","Database Migration Service moves databases and has no security posture features.","Vertex AI Studio is for prototyping with generative AI models, not security monitoring."],2],["cdl71",0,5,"An auditor asks who deleted a Compute Engine instance last Tuesday. Where would an administrator look first?",["Cloud Storage lifecycle rules","The Carbon Footprint report","Cloud Audit Logs (Admin Activity) in Cloud Logging","The Apigee developer portal"],2,"Cloud Audit Logs record administrative actions such as creating and deleting resources, including who did it and when. Admin Activity logs are always on.","Section 5.2",["Lifecycle rules change or delete Cloud Storage objects by age; they do not record who deleted a VM.","The Carbon Footprint report estimates emissions, not administrative actions.",null,"The Apigee developer portal documents APIs for developers and holds no record of Compute Engine deletions."],2],["cdl72",0,5,"A European government agency must keep data in the EU and restrict support access to EU personnel to meet a regulatory framework. Which Google Cloud offering is designed for this?",["Cloud CDN","Assured Workloads","Spot VMs","Firestore"],1,"Assured Workloads applies controls for regulated workloads, such as data location and personnel access restrictions, to help meet compliance requirements. A CDN deliberately copies content to many locations.","Section 5.3",["Cloud CDN copies content to edge locations worldwide, the opposite of keeping data in the EU.",null,"Spot VMs are discounted interruptible VMs and provide no residency or personnel controls.","Firestore is a database; choosing its location helps residency but it does not restrict support personnel."],3],["cdl73",0,5,"A customer wants to see logs showing when Google staff accessed its content and the reason for the access. Which feature provides this?",["Access Transparency","Cloud Armor","Cloud Monitoring uptime checks","Committed use discounts"],0,"Access Transparency provides logs of actions Google personnel take on customer content, with a justification. Access Approval goes further by requiring customer approval first.","Section 5.3",[null,"Cloud Armor protects web applications from attacks and does not log Google staff access.","Uptime checks test whether an endpoint responds; they do not record personnel access.","Committed use discounts are a pricing option, not a transparency feature."],3],["cdl74",0,5,"A prospective customer's compliance team asks for Google Cloud's ISO 27001 certificate and SOC 2 report. Where can it get them?",["From the Carbon Footprint page","By opening a Security Command Center finding","From Google's Compliance Reports Manager","Only by visiting a Google data center"],2,"Google publishes third-party audit reports and certificates, such as ISO/IEC 27001 and SOC reports, through its Compliance Reports Manager. Security Command Center shows the customer's own security findings.","Section 5.3",["The Carbon Footprint page reports emissions, not security certifications or audit reports.","Security Command Center findings describe risks in the customer's own environment, not Google's audit reports.",null,"Reports are downloaded online; data center visits are not how customers get certificates."],3],["cdl75",0,6,"Engineers launch resources freely, but finance only sees the total bill a month later and cannot tell which team caused a cost spike. Which practice best addresses this?",["Giving every engineer the Owner role","Moving all workloads to one region","Turning off all monitoring to save money","FinOps: shared cost visibility and accountability between finance, engineering and business teams"],3,"FinOps brings finance, technology and business teams together with shared, timely cost data and accountability. Broad roles and less monitoring would make the problem worse.","Section 6.1",["Giving everyone Owner adds security risk and does nothing to show which team caused which cost.","Consolidating regions does not give finance visibility into which team is spending.","Turning off monitoring removes visibility, the opposite of what finance needs.",null],2],["cdl76",0,6,"In the Google Cloud resource hierarchy, what does a folder usually represent?",["A single virtual machine and its attached persistent disks and network interfaces","A department or team that groups projects","A billing invoice","A storage class"],1,"Folders sit between the organization and projects and group projects, often by department, team or environment. Folders can contain other folders.","Section 6.1",["A VM and its disks are resources at the bottom of the hierarchy, not folders.",null,"Invoices come from Cloud Billing accounts; folders organize projects.","Storage classes are Cloud Storage settings and are not part of the resource hierarchy."],1],["cdl77",0,6,"Which order correctly lists the Google Cloud resource hierarchy from top to bottom?",["Project, folder, organization, resource","Organization, project, folder, resource","Resource, project, folder, organization","Organization, folder, project, resource"],3,"The organization is the root, folders group projects, projects contain resources such as VMs and buckets. Projects are also the basis for enabling APIs and linking billing.","Section 6.1",["This list is upside down; the organization is the root, not the project.","Folders sit between the organization and projects, not below projects.","This is the hierarchy reversed; resources are at the bottom, not the top.",null],1],["cdl78",0,6,"An administrator grants a group the Viewer role on a folder. What access does the group get to projects inside that folder?",["None, because roles never pass down","Viewer access to every project in the folder, through inheritance","Owner access to every project","Viewer access only to projects created after the grant"],1,"Allow policies are inherited by everything below the node where they are set, including existing and new projects. That is why broad grants high in the hierarchy should be made carefully.","Section 6.1",["Roles granted on a folder are inherited by everything below it.",null,"Inheritance passes on the role that was granted, Viewer, not a more powerful one.","Inheritance covers existing projects in the folder as well as ones created later."],2],["cdl79",0,6,"The company must ensure no one in any project can create resources outside the EU, even project owners. What is the best control?",["An organization policy restricting resource locations, set at the organization node","A label named region=eu on each project","A budget alert","An email to every team asking them to use EU regions, followed by a quarterly manual review"],0,"Organization policies set guardrails that are inherited down the hierarchy and apply regardless of IAM role, such as allowed resource locations. Labels and budgets do not block anything.","Section 6.1",[null,"A label is just a key-value tag for organizing and billing; it does not block resource creation anywhere.","A budget alert warns about spending and cannot prevent resources being created outside the EU.","An email and a later review rely on people complying and detect problems after the fact instead of preventing them."],3],["cdl80",0,6,"A team lead wants an email when a project's monthly spend reaches 80% of its planned amount. What should be configured?",["A quota on the number of VMs","An organization policy","A Cloud Billing budget with an alert threshold","A committed use discount"],2,"Budgets track actual or forecast spend and send alerts at thresholds you choose. Note that a budget alerts but does not stop spending on its own.","Section 6.1",["A quota limits resource usage such as vCPUs; it does not send spend notifications at 80% of a planned amount.","Organization policies restrict configurations such as locations; they do not track spending.",null,"A committed use discount lowers prices for steady usage but does not send alerts."],1],["cdl81",0,6,"A company wants to break down its Google Cloud costs by team and environment and analyze them with SQL. What should it do?",["Apply labels to resources and export billing data to BigQuery","Create one billing account per virtual machine so each machine's costs appear on a separate invoice","Move all resources into the Archive storage class","Disable billing reports and estimate by hand"],0,"Labels are key-value pairs that appear in billing data, and billing export to BigQuery allows detailed SQL analysis and dashboards by label. One billing account per VM is unmanageable.","Section 6.1",[null,"A billing account per VM is unmanageable and still would not group costs by team and environment.","Storage classes apply only to Cloud Storage objects and have nothing to do with cost reporting.","Disabling reports removes the data needed for analysis; estimating by hand is inaccurate."],3],["cdl82",0,6,"A company has a steady database workload that will run 24x7 for at least three years. Which pricing option usually saves the most?",["Committed use discounts for a one- or three-year term","Spot VMs","On-demand pricing with no commitment","Using a larger machine type than needed"],0,"Committed use discounts trade a one- or three-year commitment for a lower price on predictable, steady usage. Spot VMs can be stopped at any time, which does not suit a database that must stay up.","Section 6.1",[null,"Spot VMs can be stopped at any time, which does not suit a database that must run 24x7.","On-demand pricing has no commitment but misses the savings available for steady, long-term usage.","Oversizing machines increases cost rather than saving money."],2],["cdl83",0,6,"Which statement about Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) is correct?",["SRE forbids any automation of operations work","SRE is a billing model for cloud services","SRE means operations teams never talk to developers, and each group keeps its own separate goals","SRE applies software engineering to operations, for example automating toil and using SLOs"],3,"SRE, which started at Google, treats operations as a software problem: automate repetitive toil, set SLOs, use error budgets and run blameless postmortems. It is a way to implement DevOps ideas.","Section 6.2",["SRE actively automates repetitive operational work (toil); it does not forbid automation.","SRE is a way of running reliable systems, not a pricing or billing model.","SRE brings developers and operations together with shared goals such as SLOs and error budgets.",null],2],["cdl84",0,6,"A service has an SLO of 99.9% successful requests over 30 days. Which term describes the 0.1% of requests that are allowed to fail?",["The service level agreement","The service level indicator","The recovery point objective","The error budget"],3,"The error budget is the allowed unreliability, 100% minus the SLO. The SLI is the measured success rate, and the SLA is the external contract with consequences.","Section 6.2",["An SLA is the external contract with consequences, not the allowed 0.1% of failures.","The SLI is the measurement itself, such as the success rate, not the allowance for failure.","The RPO is about acceptable data loss in disaster recovery, not an SLO allowance.",null],2],["cdl85",0,6,"A cloud provider promises customers service credits if monthly uptime falls below a stated level. What is this promise?",["A service level indicator","A service level agreement","An error budget","A recovery time objective"],1,"An SLA is a contract with customers that states consequences, such as credits, when a level is missed. SLOs are internal targets and SLIs are the measurements.","Section 6.2",["An SLI is a measurement of service behavior; it carries no promise of credits.",null,"An error budget is the internal allowance of unreliability, not a customer contract.","A recovery time objective is a disaster recovery target for restoring service, not a contractual promise."],1],["cdl86",0,6,"After an outage, a business says it can lose at most 15 minutes of order data and must be running again within 2 hours. Which statement is correct?",["RPO is 2 hours and RTO is 15 minutes","Both values describe the SLA credit","RPO is 15 minutes and RTO is 2 hours","Both values describe the error budget"],2,"The recovery point objective is the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time, and the recovery time objective is how quickly service must be restored. Mixing them up is a common exam trap.","Section 6.2",["This swaps the two: data loss is measured by the RPO, and time to restore by the RTO.","SLA credits are contractual compensation, not recovery objectives for data loss and downtime.",null,"An error budget is the unreliability allowed by an SLO, not a disaster recovery target."],3],["cdl87",0,6,"An operations team wants dashboards of CPU and request latency and an alert when error rates rise, for services across several projects. Which Google Cloud service fits?",["Cloud Armor","Looker Studio","Cloud Monitoring","Cloud KMS"],2,"Cloud Monitoring collects metrics, builds dashboards, runs uptime checks and sends alerts. Cloud Logging stores and searches logs; Cloud Armor and Cloud KMS are security services.","Section 6.2",["Cloud Armor is a web application firewall and DDoS protection service, not a metrics and alerting tool.","Looker Studio builds business dashboards from data sources but is not designed for infrastructure metrics and alerting.",null,"Cloud KMS manages encryption keys and has no monitoring features."],2],["cdl88",0,6,"A developer wants to find which step of a request across several microservices is making the whole request slow. Which tool is designed for this?",["Cloud Trace","Cloud Billing reports","Compliance Reports Manager","Transfer Appliance"],0,"Cloud Trace collects latency data as requests travel between services, showing where time is spent. Logs and metrics help too, but tracing is built to follow one request end to end.","Section 6.2",[null,"Billing reports show costs, not the latency of steps within a request.","Compliance Reports Manager provides audit reports and certificates, not performance data.","Transfer Appliance is a device for moving large data sets offline."],2],["cdl89",0,6,"A company running business-critical workloads wants the fastest response to critical issues and a named technical contact who knows its environment. What should it consider?",["Relying only on community forums","A higher-tier Google Cloud Customer Care plan such as Premium","Changing all VMs to Spot VMs","Moving to a single zone"],1,"Paid Customer Care plans offer faster response times, and the top tier adds proactive services such as a Technical Account Manager. Community forums have no response commitments.","Section 6.2",["Community forums have no response commitments and no named contact for critical production issues.",null,"Spot VMs can be stopped at any time, which reduces reliability instead of improving support.","Moving to a single zone reduces resilience and does not change the level of support."],2],["cdl90",0,6,"A company wants to reduce the carbon emissions of a new workload that has no strict location requirements, and to report its cloud emissions. What should it do?",["Run the workload in every region at once","Choose a region marked as low CO2 and use the Carbon Footprint tool","Keep the workload on-premises","Use the Archive storage class for all data"],1,"Google marks regions with low grid carbon intensity in the console, and the Carbon Footprint tool reports the emissions associated with a customer's usage. Running everywhere would increase emissions.","Section 6.3",["Running in every region multiplies resource use and emissions rather than reducing them.",null,"Staying on-premises gives up Google's efficient data centers and carbon-free energy work, and the Carbon Footprint tool only reports cloud usage.","Archive storage lowers storage cost for rarely read data but is not a strategy for reducing a workload's emissions."],3],["x1",0,1,"A retailer's board says digital transformation simply means moving its servers into the cloud. Which description is MOST accurate?",["Replacing most staff roles with automated cloud services so the business can run with far fewer employees","Buying newer on-premises hardware with faster processors","Using cloud technology to change how the business operates and delivers value to customers","Moving every workload into one region to cut network costs"],2,"Digital transformation uses cloud technology to rethink processes, products and customer experiences, not just where servers sit. Replacing staff with automation is not the goal; people and culture are a central part of the change.","Domain 1: Digital transformation",["Transformation is about new ways of working and serving customers, not mainly about removing people.","Newer on-premises hardware changes nothing about how the business operates or innovates.",null,"Consolidating into one region is a narrow cost tactic and can even reduce resilience; it is not transformation."],1],["x2",0,1,"A payroll system's workload grows steadily by about 10% a year as the company hires more people, with no sudden spikes. Which cloud benefit does this MOST directly call for?",["Scalability, so capacity can grow over time to match rising demand","Elasticity for short bursts that shrink back within hours","Data sovereignty controls for the payroll records","Edge caching of payroll pages near employees"],0,"Scalability is the ability to grow capacity as demand rises over time, which matches steady yearly growth. Elasticity is about quickly adding and releasing capacity for short, changing spikes, which this workload does not have.","Domain 1: Cloud benefits",[null,"Elasticity fits short spikes that come and go; this workload grows slowly and steadily without bursts.","Data sovereignty is about where data is stored and who controls it, not about handling growth.","Edge caching speeds up delivery of content to distant users; it does not add processing capacity for growing payroll runs."],2],["x3",0,1,"A team wants virtual machines where it chooses the operating system and installs its own software, while Google manages the physical hardware and data centers. Which service model is this?",["Software as a service (SaaS)","Platform as a service (PaaS)","On-premises colocation","Infrastructure as a service (IaaS)"],3,"IaaS provides virtual machines, storage and networking; the customer controls the operating system and software while the provider runs the physical infrastructure. PaaS would hide the operating system, which this team wants to control.","Domain 1: Cloud service models",["SaaS delivers a finished application; the customer does not choose an operating system or install software on it.","PaaS manages the operating system and runtime for you, so the team could not pick the OS as it wants to.","With colocation the company still buys and manages its own physical servers in someone else's building.",null],1],["x4",0,1,"A hospital group runs virtualized, self-service infrastructure in its own data centers, and only its own departments use it. Which deployment model is this?",["Public cloud","Private cloud","Hybrid cloud","Multicloud"],1,"A private cloud offers cloud-style self-service infrastructure dedicated to one organization, here in its own data centers. It would be hybrid only if it were combined with a public cloud.","Domain 1: Deployment models",["A public cloud is shared infrastructure run by a provider such as Google, not the hospital's own data centers.",null,"Hybrid cloud combines on-premises or private infrastructure with a public cloud; no public cloud is involved here.","Multicloud means using more than one public cloud provider, which the scenario does not describe."],1],["x5",0,1,"At a company that wants to innovate faster, every new idea needs six weeks of hardware purchasing before developers can even test it. Which cloud benefit addresses this MOST directly?",["Lower network latency for customers in distant countries, because Google has points of presence worldwide","Stronger physical security at the data center","Longer depreciation periods for purchased servers","Agility, because resources can be created in minutes and removed if an experiment fails"],3,"Agility lets teams get resources on demand in minutes and discard them cheaply, so experiments are not blocked by procurement. Latency and physical security are real cloud benefits but do not remove the six-week wait.","Domain 1: Cloud benefits",["Latency affects how fast users get responses, not how long developers wait for test infrastructure.","Physical security protects hardware but does nothing to speed up getting resources for experiments.","Depreciation is an accounting matter for owned hardware; the cloud avoids the purchase entirely.",null],2],["x6",0,1,"A company must keep serving customers even if an entire Google Cloud region becomes unavailable because of a natural disaster. What should it do?",["Deploy the application in at least two regions","Spread instances across several zones in one region","Use larger machine types so each VM is more reliable","Turn on Cloud CDN and keep one backend in one zone"],0,"Only a multi-region deployment survives the loss of a whole region. Multiple zones protect against a zone failure, but all of them are in the region that went down.","Domain 1: Regions and zones",[null,"Zones in the same region protect against a single zone failure, but they are all lost if the whole region is unavailable.","A bigger VM is still a single machine in one location and fails with the region.","A CDN caches static content, but the application backend in one zone would still be unavailable."],2],["x7",0,1,"A company runs its application on Compute Engine virtual machines. Who is responsible for patching the guest operating system on those VMs?",["Google, because Compute Engine is a fully managed service","Nobody, because cloud VMs do not need operating system patches","The customer, because the guest OS is its responsibility on IaaS","The manufacturer of the physical servers"],2,"On IaaS such as Compute Engine, Google secures the hardware and hypervisor, but the customer manages the guest operating system, including patches (tools like VM Manager can help). The service being hosted by Google does not move OS patching to Google.","Domain 1: Shared responsibility",["Compute Engine is IaaS; Google runs the infrastructure but does not patch the customer's guest operating system.","Cloud VMs run ordinary operating systems that need regular security patches like any other server.",null,"Hardware makers have no role in patching a customer's guest operating system."],2],["x8",0,1,"After a migration, a CFO sees the cloud bill is higher than expected even though usage looks similar. Engineers left development VMs sized like production running around the clock. Which statement BEST explains this?",["Cloud is always more expensive than on-premises for any steady workload","Pay-as-you-go saves money only when resources are right-sized and turned off when idle","Google charges a premium for VMs that run outside business hours","Moving to the cloud removes the need for any cost governance"],1,"The cloud bills for what is provisioned, so oversized, always-on development resources waste money; savings come from right-sizing and shutting down idle resources. Cloud is not inherently more expensive for steady work, especially with committed use discounts.","Domain 1: Cloud economics",["Steady workloads can be cost-effective in the cloud, especially with committed use discounts; the issue here is waste.",null,"There is no surcharge for running outside business hours; VMs are billed for the time they run.","The opposite is true: variable cloud spending needs more cost governance, not less."],3],["x9",0,1,"What is a Google Cloud zone?",["A group of regions on the same continent","A single rack of servers in a Google data center that is reserved for one customer's workloads","A deployment area within a region, isolated from failures in the region's other zones","A billing account shared between several projects"],2,"A zone is a deployment area inside a region with its own independent infrastructure, so a failure in one zone should not affect others. A group of regions is sometimes called a multi-region or continent, not a zone.","Domain 1: Global infrastructure",["Regions are grouped by geography, but a zone is a smaller area inside a single region.","A zone contains many data center resources shared by many customers, not one rack for one customer.",null,"Billing accounts are a cost concept and have nothing to do with physical deployment areas."],1],["x10",0,1,"A manufacturer has legacy mainframe apps, a key partner that uses another cloud provider, and strict data residency rules in two countries. Which cloud strategy is MOST appropriate?",["Move every application to one Google Cloud region within a month","Stay entirely on-premises until the residency rules are removed","Rewrite every application as serverless functions first, then move all of them to the cloud together in a single cutover","A hybrid and multicloud approach built on open technologies, placing each workload where its needs are best met"],3,"Mixed requirements like legacy systems, partner integration and residency rules are best served by hybrid and multicloud, using open technologies so workloads can run where they fit. Putting everything in one region ignores the residency rules in two countries.","Domain 1: Hybrid and multicloud",["One region cannot satisfy residency rules in two countries, and a rushed move ignores the legacy systems.","Waiting for rules to disappear blocks all modernization; regional cloud options can meet residency needs.","Rewriting everything first is slow and risky, and mainframe workloads are rarely suited to functions.",null],3],["x11",0,2,"What is the main difference between an operational database and a data warehouse?",["A database handles day-to-day transactions, while a data warehouse stores large amounts of historical data for analysis","A data warehouse can only store images, video and other unstructured files","An operational database cannot be run in the cloud","A data warehouse is used only to keep backup copies of operational databases so they can be restored quickly after an outage or accidental deletion"],0,"Operational databases support frequent small reads and writes for running the business, while data warehouses such as BigQuery are built for analyzing large historical datasets. Data warehouses mostly hold structured data, not just unstructured files.","Domain 2: Databases and data warehouses",[null,"Data warehouses mainly hold structured, tabular data for analysis; unstructured files usually go to a data lake.","Operational databases run in the cloud all the time, for example on Cloud SQL or Spanner.","A data warehouse is an analytics system, not a backup store for other databases."],1],["x12",0,2,"A company wants one low-cost place to land raw data of every kind, such as logs, images and CSV exports, before deciding how each will be used. What is this BEST described as?",["A data mart built for one department","A transactional (OLTP) database","A data lake, commonly built on Cloud Storage","A semantic model in a BI tool"],2,"A data lake stores raw data of any type at low cost, and Cloud Storage is a common foundation for it on Google Cloud. A data mart is a curated, structured subset for one team, not a raw landing zone.","Domain 2: Data lakes",["A data mart holds cleaned, structured data for one team, not raw data of every type.","OLTP databases are for structured transactions, not for landing raw logs and images cheaply.",null,"A semantic model defines business metrics for reporting; it does not store raw files."],2],["x13",0,2,"An application stores each customer order as a JSON document, and the fields can differ from one order to another. What type of data is this?",["Structured data","Semi-structured data","Unstructured data","Relational data"],1,"JSON carries its own tags and structure but does not follow a fixed schema, which makes it semi-structured. It is not unstructured, because fields and values are labeled and can be queried.","Domain 2: Types of data",["Structured data follows a fixed schema of rows and columns, but these orders have varying fields.",null,"Unstructured data such as images or free text has no labeled fields, while JSON does.","Relational data lives in tables with fixed columns and relationships, which varying JSON documents are not."],2],["x14",0,2,"Disaster recovery copies in Cloud Storage are read only during quarterly DR tests and must be kept for at least a year. Which storage class is usually the BEST fit on cost?",["Standard","Nearline","Archive","Coldline"],3,"Coldline is designed for data accessed about once a quarter and has a 90-day minimum storage duration. Archive is cheaper to store but is aimed at data read less than once a year, so quarterly reads would add retrieval costs.","Domain 2: Cloud Storage classes",["Standard is for frequently accessed data and costs the most to store for rarely read copies.","Nearline suits data read about once a month, so it costs more to store than needed for quarterly access.","Archive targets data read less than once a year; quarterly reads would bring higher retrieval charges.",null],3],["x15",0,2,"BigQuery is described as serverless. What does that mean for a customer?",["Google manages the underlying infrastructure, so users load and query data without provisioning servers","Queries run on the customer's own laptops and office servers instead of inside Google's data centers, which removes cloud fees","BigQuery can only be used from inside a Compute Engine virtual machine","Data is never stored, so every query must reload its source files"],0,"Serverless means Google handles capacity, scaling and maintenance, and users simply load data and run SQL. The servers still exist, but customers do not manage them.","Domain 2: BigQuery",[null,"BigQuery runs in Google's infrastructure; serverless does not mean processing moves to the user's machine.","BigQuery is used through the console, APIs and tools; no customer VM is needed.","BigQuery stores data in managed tables; serverless refers to server management, not to storage."],1],["x16",0,2,"A small marketing team wants free, quick self-service dashboards built from Google Ads data and a spreadsheet, and does not need a centrally governed data model. Which tool fits BEST?",["Looker with a governed LookML model","Dataproc running Spark jobs","Looker Studio","Cloud Composer workflows"],2,"Looker Studio is a free, self-service tool for building dashboards from sources such as Google Ads and Sheets. Looker with LookML adds centrally governed metrics, which this small team does not need.","Domain 2: Business intelligence",["Looker's governed semantic model is powerful but more than a small team needs for quick free dashboards.","Dataproc runs Spark and Hadoop processing jobs; it does not build dashboards.",null,"Cloud Composer orchestrates data pipelines; it is not a dashboard tool."],1],["x17",0,2,"A company is building a new data pipeline from scratch and wants one fully managed service that runs both batch and streaming jobs with automatic scaling and no clusters to manage. Which service is BEST?",["Dataproc","Dataflow","Cloud SQL","Transfer Appliance"],1,"Dataflow is a serverless service for batch and streaming pipelines that scales automatically. Dataproc is best when you already have Spark or Hadoop jobs and are willing to work with clusters.","Domain 2: Data pipelines",["Dataproc is aimed at existing Spark and Hadoop workloads and is cluster-based, not the best fit for a new serverless pipeline.",null,"Cloud SQL is a managed relational database; it does not run data processing pipelines.","Transfer Appliance is a physical device for moving large data sets offline, not a processing service."],2],["x18",0,2,"A retailer wants to combine its own sales data in BigQuery with public weather data to forecast demand, without building a pipeline to copy large weather datasets. What BEST supports this?",["Exporting the weather data to CSV and emailing it to analysts","Storing the weather files in the Archive storage class and restoring them into BigQuery each time analysts need them","Loading the weather data into Memorystore","Querying BigQuery public or shared datasets directly alongside its own tables"],3,"BigQuery public datasets and shared datasets (for example through Analytics Hub) can be queried and joined in place with the company's own tables. Moving data by CSV adds work and copies that quickly go out of date.","Domain 2: Data sharing and enrichment",["Manual CSV exports are slow, error-prone and create stale copies instead of querying the data where it is.","Archive storage is for rarely read data and does not help join weather data with sales tables.","Memorystore is an in-memory cache for applications, not a place to analyze large public datasets.",null],2],["x19",0,2,"A company runs a demanding PostgreSQL workload that mixes heavy transactions with real-time analytical queries. It wants a fully managed, PostgreSQL-compatible service built for very high performance. Which is the BEST choice?",["AlloyDB for PostgreSQL","Bigtable","Cloud SQL for MySQL","Firestore"],0,"AlloyDB is a fully managed, PostgreSQL-compatible database designed for demanding transactional workloads with fast analytical queries. Cloud SQL for MySQL is managed but would force a change of database engine.","Domain 2: Managed databases",[null,"Bigtable is a NoSQL wide-column store and is not PostgreSQL-compatible.","Cloud SQL for MySQL would mean moving from PostgreSQL to a different engine, which the company wants to avoid.","Firestore is a NoSQL document database, not a PostgreSQL-compatible relational service."],3],["x20",0,2,"A company's data is spread across many Cloud Storage buckets and BigQuery datasets. It wants to discover, catalog and govern all of it centrally with consistent policies and data quality checks. Which service is designed for this?",["Cloud Run","Pub/Sub","Dataplex","Cloud CDN"],2,"Dataplex provides a central way to discover, catalog, manage and govern data across Cloud Storage and BigQuery, including data quality. Pub/Sub moves event messages between systems and does not catalog or govern data.","Domain 2: Data governance",["Cloud Run runs containerized applications; it does not catalog or govern data.","Pub/Sub is a messaging service for events, not a data catalog or governance tool.",null,"Cloud CDN caches content close to users and has no data governance features."],3],["x21",0,3,"Which task is BEST suited to machine learning rather than traditional rule-based programming?",["Adding a fixed 8% tax to every invoice total, based on the rate published by the tax authority each year","Identifying which customer emails are complaints, using thousands of labeled examples","Sorting a list of product names alphabetically","Converting distances from kilometers to miles"],1,"ML fits problems where patterns are hard to write as rules but can be learned from many examples, such as recognizing complaints in free text. A fixed tax rate is a simple known rule, so ML would add cost and uncertainty.","Domain 3: When to use ML",["A fixed percentage is a simple known rule; regular code does it exactly with no training data.",null,"Alphabetical sorting is a solved, deterministic task that needs no learning from data.","Unit conversion uses a fixed formula, so a machine learning model adds nothing."],1],["x22",0,3,"A team's ML model makes poor predictions. Reviewing the training data, they find many duplicate records, missing values and inconsistent date formats. What should the team address FIRST?",["Buy more powerful accelerators such as TPUs","Switch to a much larger model with more parameters so it can learn around the problems in the data","Deploy the model to more regions","Improve data quality so the training data is clean, complete and consistent"],3,"A model can only be as good as its data, so duplicates, gaps and inconsistent formats should be fixed first. More compute or a bigger model trained on flawed data will still learn the flaws.","Domain 3: Data quality for ML",["Faster hardware trains the same flawed data more quickly; it does not fix bad predictions.","A larger model trained on poor data still learns from the errors and may even overfit them.","Deploying in more regions changes where the model runs, not how accurate it is.",null],1],["x23",0,3,"A sales team wants AI help to summarize long email threads and draft replies inside Gmail and Docs, without building or training anything. Which option fits BEST?",["Gemini in Google Workspace","A custom model trained in Vertex AI","BigQuery ML","The Cloud Vision API"],0,"Gemini built into Google Workspace works directly in Gmail and Docs to summarize and draft content with no development effort. Custom training in Vertex AI is for unique problems and needs data science skills and time.","Domain 3: Generative AI for productivity",[null,"Custom training takes data, expertise and time, which is unnecessary for summarizing email in Gmail.","BigQuery ML builds models with SQL over data in BigQuery; it does not work inside Gmail or Docs.","The Vision API analyzes images, not email threads or documents."],1],["x24",0,3,"A call center wants to turn recorded customer calls into text so it can analyze common complaints, without building its own model. Which Google Cloud service fits BEST?",["The Cloud Translation API","The Text-to-Speech API","The Speech-to-Text API","AutoML for tabular data"],2,"Speech-to-Text is a pre-trained API that transcribes audio into text. Text-to-Speech does the reverse, turning written text into spoken audio.","Domain 3: Pre-trained APIs",["Translation converts text between languages; it does not transcribe audio.","Text-to-Speech creates spoken audio from text, which is the opposite of what the call center needs.",null,"AutoML for tabular data builds prediction models from tables, not transcriptions from audio."],1],["x25",0,3,"A retailer has no labels on its customer data but wants to group customers into segments with similar buying behavior. What type of machine learning is this?",["Supervised classification","Unsupervised learning, such as clustering","Reinforcement learning","Supervised regression"],1,"Grouping unlabeled records by similarity is unsupervised learning, typically clustering. Supervised classification needs labeled examples of each group, which the retailer does not have.","Domain 3: Types of machine learning",["Classification learns from labeled examples of each class; here there are no labels to learn from.",null,"Reinforcement learning trains an agent through rewards over actions, not grouping customers.","Regression predicts a numeric value from labeled examples; it does not discover segments."],2],["x26",0,3,"A company wants employees to ask questions in plain language and get generated answers drawn from its internal documents and websites, and it has little ML expertise. Which offering fits BEST?",["Full-text search in Cloud SQL","Spark jobs on Dataproc","Training a new large language model from scratch","Vertex AI Search in Vertex AI Agent Builder"],3,"Vertex AI Search lets companies build search and generative answers grounded in their own content with little ML work. Training a large language model from scratch would need huge data, cost and expertise the company lacks.","Domain 3: Generative AI solutions",["Database full-text search matches keywords and does not generate answers across documents and websites.","Dataproc runs data processing jobs; it is not a search or question-answering product.","Training an LLM from scratch is extremely costly and needs deep expertise, which the company does not have.",null],2],["x27",0,3,"Before using customer support transcripts to tune a model, a team finds they contain names, phone numbers and payment card numbers. Which Google Cloud service helps discover and de-identify this data?",["Sensitive Data Protection","Cloud Armor","Cloud CDN","Memorystore"],0,"Sensitive Data Protection (formerly Cloud DLP) finds sensitive data such as card numbers and can mask or de-identify it before it is used. Cloud Armor protects web applications from attacks and does not inspect training data.","Domain 3: Responsible AI and privacy",[null,"Cloud Armor is a web application firewall and DDoS protection service; it does not scan or mask data.","Cloud CDN caches web content near users and has no data inspection features.","Memorystore is an in-memory cache and does not find or de-identify sensitive data."],2],["x28",0,3,"A company wants a foundation model to write consistently in its brand voice and use its product terms. Careful prompting helps but results still vary. What is the NEXT best step that avoids building a model from scratch?",["Tune the foundation model with examples of approved content","Lower the model's safety filters so it writes more freely","Train a brand-new large language model on company data","Switch to a pre-trained image recognition API"],0,"Tuning adapts an existing foundation model with the company's own examples, improving consistency without the cost of training from scratch. Training a new LLM is far more expensive and needs much more data and expertise.","Domain 3: Customizing foundation models",[null,"Safety filters protect against harmful output; lowering them does not teach brand voice and adds risk.","Building a new LLM from scratch is extremely costly and unnecessary when a foundation model can be tuned.","An image recognition API analyzes pictures and cannot write marketing text."],3],["x29",0,3,"A fraud detection model worked well at launch, but its accuracy has slowly declined over a year as customer behavior changed. What is this called, and what should be done?",["Overfitting at launch; delete the model and stop using ML","Model drift; monitor performance and retrain on recent data","A hardware fault; move the model to TPUs","A data residency issue; move the model to another region"],1,"When real-world data changes after training, a model's accuracy drifts downward; the fix is to monitor it and retrain on current data. Overfitting shows up as poor results on new data right away, not as a slow decline after a good launch.","Domain 3: ML lifecycle",["Overfitting would cause poor results on new data from the start, not a gradual decline after good performance.",null,"Hardware affects speed, not the accuracy of predictions as behavior changes.","Where the model runs does not change how well it matches current customer behavior."],3],["x30",0,3,"A telecom company wants virtual agents that handle common customer calls and chats, and hand off to human agents with the conversation context when needed. Which Google Cloud solution area fits?",["Cloud Monitoring uptime checks","BigQuery scheduled queries that run SQL against call records every hour and email the results to agents","Storage Transfer Service","Conversational AI, such as Customer Engagement Suite with Dialogflow"],3,"Google's conversational AI offerings, such as Customer Engagement Suite with Dialogflow, provide virtual agents for voice and chat that can escalate to humans with context. Monitoring and data transfer tools do not talk to customers.","Domain 3: AI solutions for customer service",["Uptime checks watch service availability; they do not interact with customers.","Scheduled queries run SQL on a timetable and do not handle customer conversations.","Storage Transfer Service moves data between storage systems, not customer calls.",null],2],["x31",0,4,"What is serverless computing on Google Cloud?",["Running applications on physical servers the customer owns in its own data center","Renting virtual machines and managing their operating systems, patches, capacity and scaling yourself","Running code or containers while Google manages servers and scaling, paying only for what is used","A pricing plan that removes all charges for small applications"],2,"With serverless services like Cloud Run, Google provisions and scales the infrastructure, and customers pay for actual use. Renting VMs and managing them yourself is IaaS, not serverless.","Domain 4: Serverless computing",["Owning physical servers is the opposite of serverless; the customer manages everything.","Managing VMs, their OS and scaling yourself describes IaaS such as Compute Engine.",null,"Serverless is a way of running code, not a pricing plan; some usage is free but it is not free in general."],1],["x32",0,4,"A company rewrites a legacy application as containerized microservices on GKE so it can use cloud-native features. Which migration approach is this?",["Refactor (re-architect)","Rehost (lift and shift)","Retire","Retain"],0,"Refactoring changes the application's architecture and code to become cloud-native, such as splitting it into microservices. Rehosting moves the application as it is, without code changes.","Domain 4: Migration approaches",[null,"Rehosting moves an application unchanged to cloud VMs, while here the code and architecture are rewritten.","Retiring means shutting the application down, not rebuilding it.","Retaining keeps the application where it is for now, with no migration."],1],["x33",0,4,"Before migrating, a company wants to discover its on-premises servers, estimate what they would cost to run on Google Cloud and plan migration waves. Which Google Cloud tool is designed for this?",["Cloud Deploy","Security Command Center","Artifact Registry","Migration Center"],3,"Migration Center helps discover and assess on-premises assets, estimate cloud costs and plan the migration. Cloud Deploy automates application releases and does not assess existing servers.","Domain 4: Migration planning",["Cloud Deploy delivers application releases to targets; it does not inventory on-premises servers.","Security Command Center reports security risks in Google Cloud, not migration costs.","Artifact Registry stores container images and packages; it does not assess servers.",null],2],["x34",0,4,"Why do modern applications expose their functions through APIs?",["APIs make every application run faster on any hardware, because requests no longer need to be processed by servers","Other systems and partners can integrate through a defined contract without knowing the internal code","APIs remove the need for any authentication","APIs let the application skip testing"],1,"An API is a defined contract that lets other systems use a service without depending on how it is built inside, which speeds integration and reuse. APIs still need authentication and security controls.","Domain 4: APIs",["APIs are about integration, not about making code run faster on hardware.",null,"APIs should be protected with authentication and authorization, not exempt from it.","APIs need testing like any other code; exposing an API does not remove that."],1],["x35",0,4,"A company runs dozens of containerized services with complex networking, custom scheduling needs and some stateful workloads, and its platform team has strong Kubernetes skills. Which is the BEST fit?",["Cloud Run functions","App Engine standard environment","Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)","Bare Metal Solution"],2,"GKE offers the full Kubernetes control needed for complex networking, custom scheduling and stateful workloads, and the team has the skills to use it. Cloud Run functions suit small event-driven pieces of code, not a large platform of services.","Domain 4: Containers and GKE",["Functions are for small event-driven code, not dozens of services with custom scheduling needs.","App Engine standard runs web apps in set runtimes and does not give Kubernetes-level control.",null,"Bare Metal Solution provides dedicated physical servers for specialized workloads, not container orchestration."],2],["x36",0,4,"For compliance and licensing reasons, a company must run its Compute Engine VMs on physical servers used only by its own projects, while still using Compute Engine. What should it use?",["Spot VMs","Sole-tenant nodes","Bare Metal Solution","Shielded VMs"],1,"Sole-tenant nodes are physical Compute Engine servers dedicated to one customer's VMs. Bare Metal Solution also gives dedicated hardware, but it is a separate offering for specialized workloads rather than Compute Engine VMs.","Domain 4: Compute options",["Spot VMs are low-cost, interruptible capacity on shared hardware, not dedicated servers.",null,"Bare Metal Solution gives dedicated physical servers without the Compute Engine VM service the company wants to keep.","Shielded VMs harden boot integrity but still run on shared hosts."],3],["x37",0,4,"A team wants every code change to be built, tested and deployed to Cloud Run automatically through a pipeline. Which set of Google Cloud services supports this?",["Cloud Storage Archive class and Transfer Appliance for moving build outputs between environments","Pub/Sub and Bigtable","Cloud Armor and Cloud KMS","Cloud Build and Cloud Deploy, with images stored in Artifact Registry"],3,"Cloud Build runs builds and tests, Artifact Registry stores the resulting images, and Cloud Deploy manages releases to targets such as Cloud Run. Cloud Armor and Cloud KMS are security services, not CI/CD tools.","Domain 4: DevOps and CI/CD",["These services store and move data; they do not build or deploy code.","Pub/Sub and Bigtable handle messaging and data storage, not build pipelines.","Cloud Armor protects web apps and Cloud KMS manages keys; neither builds or deploys code.",null],2],["x38",0,4,"An organization wants to create identical development, test and production environments in a repeatable way, and review infrastructure changes the same way it reviews code. Which approach fits BEST?",["Creating resources by hand in the console and documenting the steps in a wiki","Copying production VMs by hand every quarter","Infrastructure as code, for example with Terraform","Granting every developer the Owner role so they can fix differences"],2,"Infrastructure as code defines resources in version-controlled files, so environments are reproducible and changes can be reviewed. Manual console work drifts between environments and is hard to audit.","Domain 4: Infrastructure as code",["Manual console steps are error-prone and environments drift apart even with good documentation.","Manual quarterly copies are slow, inconsistent and cannot be reviewed like code changes.",null,"Broad Owner access violates least privilege and makes environments less consistent, not more."],2],["x39",0,4,"A factory needs to run Google Cloud-managed infrastructure and services on hardware at its own site, because it needs very low latency and some data cannot leave the premises. Which offering is designed for this?",["Google Distributed Cloud","Cloud CDN","Cloud Run in a nearby region","Spanner multi-region configuration"],0,"Google Distributed Cloud brings Google Cloud infrastructure and services to customer sites and edge locations for low latency and data that must stay on premises. Running in a nearby cloud region still sends data off-site.","Domain 4: Hybrid and edge",[null,"Cloud CDN caches content for users; it does not run workloads on factory hardware.","A nearby region is still off-site, so data would leave the premises.","A multi-region Spanner database runs in Google data centers, not at the factory."],3],["x40",0,4,"Why might a company modernize a large application gradually, moving one function at a time into microservices, instead of rewriting it all at once?",["Full rewrites of production applications are not permitted on Google Cloud under its terms of service","It lowers risk and delivers value sooner, because each piece can be tested and released on its own","Gradual changes remove the need for testing","Microservices can only be created one per month"],1,"Incremental modernization limits the blast radius of each change and delivers benefits along the way, while a big-bang rewrite delays value and concentrates risk. Every change still needs testing.","Domain 4: Application modernization",["Google Cloud does not forbid rewrites; the choice is about risk and value, not permission.",null,"Each step still needs testing; gradual change makes testing smaller, not unnecessary.","There is no limit on how quickly microservices can be created."],2],["x41",0,5,"An employee's password was stolen, but the attacker still could not sign in because the account also requires a physical security key. Which control stopped the attacker?",["Encryption at rest of the account's stored data with Google-managed keys","Data loss prevention","Two-step verification (multi-factor authentication)","VPC firewall rules"],2,"Two-step verification requires something beyond the password, such as a security key, so a stolen password alone is not enough. Encryption at rest protects stored data but does not stop someone from signing in.","Domain 5: Identity and authentication",["Encryption at rest protects stored data on disk; it does not block sign-ins with stolen passwords.","Data loss prevention finds and protects sensitive data; it does not verify identity at sign-in.",null,"Firewall rules control network traffic, not whether a stolen password can be used to sign in."],1],["x42",0,5,"A new analyst needs to run queries on one BigQuery dataset. To save time, an administrator proposes granting the Owner role on the whole project. Which principle does this violate?",["Least privilege","Defense in depth","Separation of environments","Data residency"],0,"Least privilege means giving only the access needed for the task, here a BigQuery role on one dataset. Defense in depth is about layering multiple controls, not about sizing a single user's access.","Domain 5: Least privilege",[null,"Defense in depth layers several controls; the specific problem here is granting far more access than needed.","Separating environments keeps dev and production apart; the issue here is excessive permissions.","Data residency is about where data is stored, not how much access a user gets."],1],["x43",0,5,"How does Google Cloud protect customer data as it travels between Google's data centers?",["Only if the customer installs and maintains VPN software on every virtual machine that sends data between regions","It is sent in plain text because Google's network is private","Compression is applied, which also acts as encryption","Data in transit is encrypted, and traffic between Google facilities stays largely on Google's private network"],3,"Google encrypts data in transit between its data centers and carries it over its own private backbone network. Compression reduces size but provides no confidentiality.","Domain 5: Encryption in transit",["Google encrypts traffic between its data centers by default; customers do not need a VPN on each VM for this.","Google does not rely on network privacy alone; data moving between its facilities is encrypted.","Compression makes data smaller but anyone can decompress it, so it is not encryption.",null],2],["x44",0,5,"A company wants to manage users, groups and single sign-on for Google Cloud centrally, and keep them in sync with its existing Microsoft Active Directory. What should it use?",["A separate service account for every employee","Cloud Identity with Google Cloud Directory Sync","Cloud KMS key rings for each department","VPC firewall rules for each user"],1,"Cloud Identity manages users and groups for Google Cloud, and Google Cloud Directory Sync keeps them aligned with Active Directory. Service accounts are identities for workloads, not for people.","Domain 5: Identity management",["Service accounts are meant for applications and workloads, not for employees signing in.",null,"Cloud KMS manages encryption keys; it does not manage users or single sign-on.","Firewall rules control network traffic and cannot manage user identities."],3],["x45",0,5,"An application running on Compute Engine needs to write files to one Cloud Storage bucket. What is the recommended way to give it access?",["Attach a service account that has only the needed Cloud Storage role on that bucket","Put a developer's personal username and password in the application's configuration file so it can sign in","Make the bucket publicly writable","Grant allUsers the Editor role on the project"],0,"Workloads should use a service account granted only the roles they need, which avoids embedded credentials and follows least privilege. Making the bucket public would let anyone on the internet write to it.","Domain 5: Service accounts",[null,"Personal passwords in code can leak and tie the app to one person's access; workloads should use service accounts.","A publicly writable bucket lets anyone on the internet upload or overwrite data.","Giving allUsers Editor exposes the whole project to anyone and violates least privilege."],2],["x46",0,5,"Which of the following is the MOST common cause of data exposure incidents in the cloud?",["Physical theft of disks from the provider's data centers","Weaknesses in the cryptographic algorithms the provider uses to encrypt data","Customer misconfigurations, such as storage or access set too permissively","Damage to undersea network cables"],2,"Most cloud data exposures come from customer-side misconfiguration, such as public buckets or overly broad permissions. Physical theft from hyperscale data centers is rare because of strict physical security and encryption at rest.","Domain 5: Cloud security risks",["Provider data centers have strong physical controls and encrypt data at rest, so disk theft is rarely the cause.","Standard encryption algorithms are not the usual weak point; configuration mistakes are.",null,"Cable damage can affect availability but does not typically expose data."],2],["x47",0,5,"A security team wants to ingest very large volumes of security telemetry, search a year of it quickly and automate response playbooks. Which Google Cloud offering is designed for this?",["Cloud Monitoring uptime checks","Cloud Deploy","Looker Studio","Google Security Operations"],3,"Google Security Operations combines SIEM-style ingestion and fast search over large security data with SOAR playbooks for automated response. Cloud Monitoring tracks performance and availability, not security investigations.","Domain 5: Security operations",["Uptime checks confirm that a service responds; they do not analyze security telemetry.","Cloud Deploy releases applications and has no security analytics features.","Looker Studio builds dashboards but does not ingest security telemetry at scale or run response playbooks.",null],3],["x48",0,5,"A research firm wants its sensitive data to stay encrypted even while it is being processed in memory on virtual machines. Which Google Cloud capability addresses this?",["Customer-managed encryption keys for Cloud Storage","Confidential VMs (Confidential Computing)","Cloud Armor security policies","VPC Service Controls perimeters"],1,"Confidential VMs encrypt data in use, while it is in memory and being processed. Customer-managed keys control encryption of stored data at rest, not data in memory.","Domain 5: Data protection",["CMEK controls the keys for data at rest in storage; it does not protect data while it is being processed in memory.",null,"Cloud Armor protects web applications from attacks, not data in VM memory.","VPC Service Controls reduce data exfiltration between services but do not encrypt memory."],3],["x49",0,5,"Developers have been storing database passwords and API keys directly in source code. Which Google Cloud service should they use instead?",["Secret Manager","Cloud Logging","Artifact Registry","Cloud Storage with public access"],0,"Secret Manager stores secrets such as passwords and API keys centrally, with IAM access control, versioning and audit logging. Artifact Registry stores build artifacts and is not meant for secrets.","Domain 5: Secrets management",[null,"Cloud Logging collects logs; writing secrets into logs would expose them further.","Artifact Registry holds container images and packages, not credentials.","A publicly accessible bucket would expose the secrets to anyone on the internet."],2],["x50",0,5,"Google Cloud holds ISO 27001 certification and SOC 2 reports. An auditor asks whether a customer's own workloads on Google Cloud are therefore compliant. What is correct?",["Yes, customers automatically inherit full compliance for every workload they deploy, so no further controls, configuration or evidence are needed from them","Compliance matters only to the auditor, not to the customer","Google's certifications cover its infrastructure and services; the customer must still configure and operate its workloads compliantly","The certifications apply only to Google's on-premises products"],2,"Google's certifications show that its infrastructure and services meet the standards, but under shared responsibility the customer must still configure, control and document its own workloads. Customers do not inherit full compliance automatically.","Domain 5: Compliance",["Customers can rely on Google's controls for the infrastructure but must still meet their own control obligations.","Compliance is the customer's obligation; the auditor only assesses it.",null,"These certifications cover Google Cloud services, not only on-premises products."],2],["x51",0,6,"What is a Google Cloud project?",["A single virtual machine and its attached disks","The basic container for resources, where APIs are enabled, billing is linked and IAM access is set","A group of organizations that share one billing account and a single set of IAM policies across all of them","A physical data center assigned to one customer"],1,"Projects hold resources and are where APIs are enabled, a billing account is linked and IAM policies apply. Folders and the organization sit above projects in the hierarchy.","Domain 6: Resource hierarchy",["A VM is one resource that lives inside a project, not the project itself.",null,"Organizations are at the top of the hierarchy; projects are below them, not groups of them.","Projects are logical containers, not physical data centers."],1],["x52",0,6,"A developer needs to search the error messages that a Cloud Run service wrote yesterday. Which tool should they use?",["Cloud Trace","Cloud Billing reports","Recommender","Cloud Logging"],3,"Cloud Logging collects and lets you search logs from Cloud Run and other services. Cloud Trace shows request latency across services, not the text of error messages.","Domain 6: Logging",["Cloud Trace shows where time is spent in requests; it is not the place to search log messages.","Billing reports show spending, not application error messages.","Recommender suggests optimizations; it does not store logs.",null],1],["x53",0,6,"An administrator wants suggestions for idle VMs to delete and oversized VMs to shrink, based on how they are actually used. What provides this?",["Recommender (Active Assist) recommendations","Cloud Armor security policies attached to the load balancer","Access Transparency logs","Cloud CDN cache reports"],0,"Recommender, part of Active Assist, analyzes usage and suggests actions such as deleting idle VMs or resizing oversized ones. Access Transparency logs Google staff access to data and has nothing to do with right-sizing.","Domain 6: Cost optimization",[null,"Cloud Armor protects web applications; it does not analyze VM utilization.","Access Transparency records Google staff access to customer content, not resource usage.","CDN reports show caching behavior, not VM sizing opportunities."],2],["x54",0,6,"A Compute Engine VM of an eligible machine type runs for most of the month, and the company has made no commitment. Which discount can apply automatically?",["A committed use discount","Spot VM pricing","A sustained use discount","A free tier credit for the whole VM"],2,"Sustained use discounts apply automatically to eligible machine types that run for a large part of the billing month. Committed use discounts require buying a one- or three-year commitment first.","Domain 6: Pricing options",["Committed use discounts need a one- or three-year commitment, which the company has not made.","Spot pricing applies only to Spot VMs that can be stopped at any time, not to a normal VM.",null,"The free tier covers only small usage amounts, not a VM running most of the month."],2],["x55",0,6,"A team set a Cloud Billing budget of $5,000 with alert thresholds, yet spending reached $7,000 that month. Why did the budget not stop the spending?",["Budgets apply only to the following billing month","Budgets send alerts but do not cap spending on their own; automation is needed to take action","The budget was overridden by committed use discounts","Budgets work only for BigQuery analysis charges, so Compute Engine and other service costs were never counted"],1,"Cloud Billing budgets notify people when thresholds are reached but do not stop resources; to act automatically, budget notifications can trigger code through Pub/Sub. Committed use discounts lower prices and do not override budgets.","Domain 6: Budgets and cost control",["Budgets track the current period; the issue is that they alert rather than block spending.",null,"Committed use discounts reduce costs; they have nothing to do with whether a budget stops spending.","Budgets can cover all services linked to a billing account, not only BigQuery."],3],["x56",0,6,"Which design BEST improves the resilience of a stateless web application on Google Cloud?",["Run it on one very large VM","Take weekly snapshots of a single VM","Put all instances in a single zone so traffic between them has the lowest possible latency","Run instances in multiple zones behind a load balancer with health checks"],3,"Spreading instances across zones behind a load balancer with health checks keeps the app serving if a VM or a zone fails. Weekly snapshots help with recovery, but the app would still be down until someone restored it.","Domain 6: Reliability and resilience",["A single large VM is a single point of failure, however powerful it is.","Snapshots help rebuild after failure but do not keep the app running during one.","One zone means a zone outage takes down every instance at once.",null],2],["x57",0,6,"Engineers spend hours every week manually restarting the same service after predictable failures. In Site Reliability Engineering, what is this work called, and what should be done?",["Toil; automate it and fix the underlying cause","An error budget; spend it on new features","A service level indicator; publish it to customers","A postmortem; hold one for each restart"],0,"SRE calls manual, repetitive, automatable operational work toil and aims to reduce it through automation and fixing root causes. An error budget is the allowed amount of unreliability, not a name for repetitive work.","Domain 6: Site Reliability Engineering",[null,"An error budget is the unreliability allowed by an SLO, not repetitive manual work.","An SLI is a measurement of service behavior, not a type of operational work.","Postmortems analyze significant incidents; holding one per routine restart does not remove the toil."],2],["x58",0,6,"After a major outage, a manager asks which engineer should be blamed. What does SRE practice recommend instead?",["Disciplining whoever made the last change","Skipping any formal review so the team can move on quickly and get back to delivering new features","A blameless postmortem focused on systemic causes and follow-up actions","Freezing all changes permanently"],2,"Blameless postmortems focus on how systems and processes allowed the failure and what will prevent a repeat, so people report problems openly. Blaming individuals discourages honesty and leaves the underlying causes in place.","Domain 6: Site Reliability Engineering",["Punishing individuals discourages people from reporting problems and ignores systemic causes.","Skipping review means the team does not learn and the same failure can recur.",null,"A permanent freeze stops all improvement; SRE balances change and reliability instead."],2],["x59",0,6,"A team measures the percentage of requests that are served in under 300 milliseconds. What is this measurement called?",["A service level objective (SLO)","A service level indicator (SLI)","A service level agreement (SLA)","An error budget"],1,"An SLI is the measured metric itself, such as the share of fast requests. An SLO is the target set for that metric, for example 99% of requests under 300 ms.","Domain 6: SLIs and SLOs",["An SLO is a target value for an SLI, such as 99%; the measurement itself is the SLI.",null,"An SLA is a contract with customers that usually includes consequences for missing targets.","The error budget is the allowed shortfall from the SLO, not the measurement."],3],["x60",0,6,"In a new project, an attempt to create 200 VMs fails, even though billing is active and the user has the correct IAM role. What is the MOST likely cause?",["A budget alert on the billing account automatically blocked the request when the forecast crossed 100%","Access Transparency denied the request","The VMs require customer-managed encryption keys","The project reached a resource quota, which can be raised by request"],3,"Projects have quotas that limit how many resources, such as CPUs or VMs, can be created; a large request can exceed them, and increases can be requested. Budget alerts only send notifications and do not block creation.","Domain 6: Quotas and limits",["Budget alerts notify people but do not stop resource creation by themselves.","Access Transparency logs Google staff access; it does not approve or deny customer requests.","CMEK is optional; not using it does not block VM creation.",null],3]]);
